# TAC-7 Nuggets — ranked for the struggling learner

Ordering rule: how much of the lesson collapses if this nugget is missing. Tier 1 is the tactic, the scale it lives on, and the secret it ends on; Tier 2 is the machinery that earns the right to drop your presence; Tier 3 is the posture that keeps ZTE a disciplined target instead of a reckless YOLO. This is the second-to-last lesson — nothing here is new machinery, it's TAC-4/5/6 composed and run in parallel, with the last human step removed.

---

## Tier 1 — If you remember nothing else

**1. Tactic #7 is Target Zero-Touch Engineering — and the word "Target" is load-bearing.**
TAC-1 told you to stop coding. TAC-7 comes for the thing you replaced it with: reviewing. ZTE = presence drops from two touchpoints (prompt + review) to one (prompt only); work flows prompt → production, auto-merged, untouched. But it's a North Star you *target* one problem class at a time (chores → bugs → features), not a switch you flip on day one. "This is the ultimate agentic coding North Star." Missing the word "Target" is how students hear "auto-merge everything" and correctly conclude that's insane.

**2. The Agentic Engineering Velocity Scale is a gradient, not a switch: In-Loop → Out-Loop → ZTE.**
Dan's exact framing: "It's not in loop versus out loop. It's in loop moving toward out loop." He *deliberately* steps back in-loop (22:59) to hand-review the drag-and-drop PR. You move along the scale per-problem-class, "one day at a time" — never a global mode flip. If you think of these as three settings on a dial you turn once, you've misread the spine of the whole lesson.

**3. Presence is the one number that tracks the entire course arc.**
It's the KPI under both TAC-1 and TAC-7: how many human touchpoints per shipped unit. In-loop = continuous; out-loop best = 2 (prompt, then review + merge); ZTE = 1 (prompt only). TAC-1 removed typing; TAC-7 removes reviewing; presence is what goes down both times. When you can't decide whether a run "counts" as ZTE, you're really asking what its presence was.
*Test yourself:* open `app_docs/agentic_kpis.md` — a real committed run (cc73faf1, issue #47, /chore) shows `average presence 1.0, streak 1`. If you can't read that row as "a real ZTE ship already happened in this repo," the KPI isn't real to you yet.

**4. The secret: it was never about the SDLC. It's composable agentic primitives.**
Stated verbatim, twice: "The secret of tactical agentic coding is that it's not about the software developer lifecycle at all. It's about composable agentic primitives you can use to solve any engineering problem class." The slide (t2765) reads Agentic Primitives → ADW steps → **ANY WORKFLOW**. This isn't a rug-pull: the SDLC still works, it's just *one arbitrary arrangement* — "a package for me to deliver a message." The failure is enshrining any fixed arrangement instead of owning the primitives and recomposing per problem class.

---

## Tier 2 — The machinery that earns the presence drop

**5. Git worktrees (or Docker, or E2B) — N agents, one device, zero collisions. The tool is not the point.**
Every workflow in tac-7 is `_iso`: it runs in `trees/<adw_id>/`, a *complete copy of the app* with deterministic ports (base-36 hash of the ADW ID % 15 → backend 9100–9114, frontend 9200–9214, socket-checked with fallback). That's how five agents run in parallel without fighting over branch, dev server, or DB. But Dan pre-empts worktree fixation twice: "I don't care how you scale up your agent environments, it only matters that you can." The repo even ships an E2B cloud-sandbox PoC. Fixating on worktrees-specifically is missing the concept for the implementation.

**6. The ADW state object is pipeline memory that lives OUTSIDE the context window.**
`agents/<adw_id>/adw_state.json`, ten load-bearing fields (adw_id, issue_number, branch_name, plan_file, issue_class, worktree_path, backend_port, frontend_port, model_set, all_adws). Every phase does load → contribute → save, so a fresh single-purpose agent in phase 5 knows the branch phase 1 created without you re-prompting it. Stuffing that bookkeeping into the agent's context instead breaks TAC-6's one-agent-one-prompt-one-purpose ("has not been broken. This is really important for scaling") and makes runs unreplayable.

**7. Model sets are compute allocation encoded as one information-dense keyword.**
Type `model_set heavy` in the issue → `/classify_adw` parses it into state → each `execute_template` resolves its model from `SLASH_COMMAND_MODEL_MAP` in `agents.py`. On heavy, exactly **8 of 18** commands escalate sonnet→opus (implement, resolve_failed_test, resolve_failed_e2e_test, document, chore, bug, feature, patch); classification/branch/test/review/commit/PR stay sonnet. The map's *selectivity* is the lesson. `model_set heavy` on everything "to be safe" = paying opus prices to extract JSON.

**8. Review = proof of value, and it's the trigger for everything downstream (including ZTE).**
Review is not re-testing. Testing asks "does it work?"; review asks "is what we built what we asked for?" — and it *proves it*: the review agent boots the app in its own worktree, drives it with Playwright MCP, takes 1–5 numbered screenshots of critical paths, classifies findings `skippable / tech_debt / blocker` (only **blocker** flips `success=false`), uploads to R2, posts to the issue, and auto-patches blockers ("attempt 1/3"). Dropping your review presence is only legitimate *because* this proof lands without you. Never opening the screenshots is how you fool yourself into ZTE.
*Test yourself:* three severity levels — which one blocks? *(Only `blocker`. If you said "all of them," your review will cry wolf and never let you reach presence 1.)*

**9. The ZTE pipeline is engineered to REFUSE to merge — count the gates before you fear it.**
`adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py` = SDLC + ship. Test failure → "ZTE Aborted." Review failure → "ZTE Aborted." Documentation failure → warn but continue ("shouldn't block shipping"). Ship itself: validate all 8 state fields non-null → validate the worktree three ways → in the repo root fetch → checkout main → pull → `git merge --no-ff` → push, *restoring the original branch on every failure path*. And upstream: `/classify_adw` refuses `adw_sdlc_ZTE_iso` unless **ZTE is literally uppercase** — lowercase silently downgrades to reviewed SDLC. "Agents auto-merging to main" is guarded at five distinct points, not zero.

**10. Both SDLC orchestrators hardcode `--skip-e2e`. Do not assume the browser E2E ran.**
Unit tests gate the pipeline; browser E2E is a separate standalone run (`adw_test_iso.py` without `--skip-e2e`). Assuming E2E ran on your ZTE ship is exactly the kind of unverified belief the lesson trains you out of. (Verified in source, both `adw_sdlc_iso.py` and `adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py`.)

---

## Tier 3 — Posture that keeps ZTE disciplined, not reckless

**11. ZTE is earned per problem class, and the "toy app" objection is named and answered in-lesson.**
Dan says it for you (32:37): "this isn't a real production application, you're pushing way too far." He agrees the app is a toy, then redirects: start with chores → bugs → features, and *target* ZTE so you discover when it's real for each class — "how else will you know unless you're trying?" The example being a background-color change is the point, not a weakness: ZTE is earned on exactly this low-stakes class first.

**12. Nothing in lesson 7 is new. The delta is scale + removing the last human.**
Five pieces of work shipped in parallel from six minutes of issue-writing is not new machinery — it's TAC-4's PITER + TAC-5's loops + TAC-6's focused agents, *composed and run in parallel*. "Once you've built the system that builds the system, it's going to pay you back massively." If you're hunting for the new trick, there isn't one; there's composition and the courage to drop the R.

**13. Verify countable claims against source, not the README. Docs drift; code ships.**
`adws/README.md` says ship "approves the PR, squash method." The code does a manual `git merge --no-ff` + push — and Dan's *narration* matches the code, not the README. Same lesson as the uppercase-`zte` interlock: if you type lowercase and it downgrades, that's the safety working, not a bug. When a claim is countable (how many workflows, which merge strategy, did E2E run), read the source.

**14. The real blocker is belief, not technology — and the lesson's ask is one concrete step.**
"The real challenge... is about your belief. You have to believe that the agentic layer is worth investing into... If you don't believe that your agents can run your codebase, you're cooked." "It's not about being right. It's about being prepared to win." Consuming this as motivation-content and changing nothing is the failure. The ask: pick one chore class this week, encode it, instrument it, and let the KPIs argue.

**15. Own the primitives; the arrangement is yours. "The one way is the way that ships in your codebase."**
"This arrangement of ADWs is completely arbitrary." The 14 workflow scripts are just *compositions* of the same phase modules — the CSV bug took `patch`, the features took `sdlc`, on identical infrastructure. Every org's arrangement "is going to look a bit different." Hearing "SDLC is dead, delete my pipeline" is the misread; the transferable skill is recomposing primitives per problem class.

---

## Micro-quiz (close the book first)

1. Name the three levels of the velocity scale and your presence count at each. Why is it a gradient, not a switch — and what did Dan do at 22:59 that proves it?
2. Why is the word "Target" in the tactic name load-bearing? What sequence of problem classes does Dan give for approaching ZTE?
3. State the secret in one sentence. If it's true, why isn't "seven lessons of SDLC" a rug-pull?
4. Give the ZTE pipeline's gates in order: which failing phase does NOT abort the ship, and what runs before any git command in the ship step? What silently downgrades a ZTE run?
5. `model_set heavy` — which phase reads it off the raw issue, where is it stored, and how many of the 18 commands actually escalate to opus?
6. Review found no blockers, only `tech_debt`. Did the pipeline ship? What did review compare against — and why is that a different question from "did the tests pass"?

*(Answers: extract.md ideas 2, 8, 11, 9, 5, 6.)*
# TAC-7 Lab Spec — ZTE: The Secret of Agentic Engineering

**Scope rule:** every drill runs inside the course codebase only (`codebase/` → `tac/tac-7`; students: `git clone https://github.com/disler/tac-7.git`). You additionally need a GitHub repo **you control** — the ADW pipeline IS issues, comments, and PRs. No external repos, no new machinery; every workflow in tac-7 is already `_iso`.
**Prime directive (graded throughout):** you type **zero** lines of application code by hand, AND you record your **presence** honestly for every run (how many times you touched it: prompt only = 1, prompt + review = 2, anything more = you were in-loop). The point of this lab is to *watch your own presence fall per problem class* — not to reach ZTE on run one.
**Time box:** 2–3 hours (the capstone lab). Token cost is real and the highest of the course: multiple full plan→build→test→review→document runs, one on `model_set heavy` (opus), plus a ship. Budget for it; running out mid-ship and blaming ZTE is the failure the pay-to-play lesson is designed to surface.

---

## 0 · Setup (20 min)

1. Clone the lesson repo: `git clone https://github.com/disler/tac-7.git && cd tac-7`. **Own the review surface:** create your own GitHub repo, `git remote remove origin && git remote add origin <your-repo> && git push -u origin main` (Dan does the equivalent on-video by resetting origin). `gh auth status` must be green.
2. Copy `.env.sample` → `.env`; set `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` (the headless billing path) and `export GITHUB_REPO_URL`. Fill `app/server/.env` for the NL→SQL app's own key. **Optional but demo-faithful:** the four Cloudflare R2 vars so review screenshots render as images on the issue — without them you get local file paths, and **that is not an error** (the uploader disables silently).
3. Verify the toolchain: `claude --version`, `gh auth status`, `uv --version`, `bun --version`, `git --version` (must support worktrees).
4. Boot the app once yourself: `./scripts/start.sh` → `http://localhost:5173`; upload sample data; run one NL query; `./scripts/stop_apps.sh`. You need to know what "working" looks like before an agent ships to it.
5. Read `.claude/settings.json` and `adws/README.md`. Say out loud, before you run anything: what may a workflow do, and where will its evidence land? *(Answer you're building toward: `agents/<adw_id>/` for state + logs, `trees/<adw_id>/` for the isolated app copy, and the issue thread for every human-visible report.)*

**Checkpoint 0:** you can name the three places evidence lands and state why review/patch/ship need a repo you own — their output *is* issue comments, PR updates, and a merge.

## 1 · One issue → a production-shaped run (30 min)

1. File a GitHub issue in Dan's shape: `Add a footer showing the current table count. adw_sdlc_iso`. Run it manually first (webhook comes later): `cd adws && uv run adw_sdlc_iso.py <issue#>`. Hands off the repo the moment it starts.
2. While it runs, **prove the isolation with your own eyes:** `git worktree list` shows `trees/<adw_id>`; `cat trees/<adw_id>/.ports.env` shows the deterministic 9100-/9200-range pair; `cat agents/<adw_id>/adw_state.json` — name all **ten** fields aloud.
3. Jump into the agent's environment mid-run, like Dan: `code trees/<adw_id>` and watch the branch in the status bar. You are a guest in the agent's world, not a co-editor — do not touch files.
4. When it finishes, audit the evidence chain top-to-bottom on the issue: classification → state JSON → plan path → test summary → review summary **with ≥1 screenshot** → documentation → the PR. Open the PR diff.

**Checkpoint 1:** the issue thread reads as a complete paper trail, and you can point at where each of the ten state fields got populated. Record your presence for this run.

## 2 · Go in-loop on purpose (15 min)

1. This is the beat most students skip. In `claude`, run `/in_loop_review <branch-name>`; it fetches + checks out the branch, resets/starts the app, and opens `localhost` for hands-on inspection.
2. Exercise the footer feature yourself in the browser. Write one sentence: **what did your review catch?** (Be honest — on a clean run, likely nothing.)

**Checkpoint 2:** you can explain Dan's "in loop moving toward out loop" from experience — you *chose* to add a touchpoint, and you can say whether it added value. When it stops adding value for this class, that's the signal to drop it.

## 3 · Turn the compute knob (20 min)

1. New issue, heavier work: `Generate random rows for a selected table. adw_sdlc_iso model_set heavy`. Run it.
2. **Prove the knob actually turned:** find which model the implementor ran — `agents/<adw_id>/sdlc_implementor/raw_output.jsonl` (the `model` field) or the "Model Set" line in the issue thread. Confirm the implementor escalated to **opus** while classification/branch/test stayed on **sonnet**.

**Checkpoint 3:** you can state that `model_set` is one IDK read by `/classify_adw`, stored in `adw_state.json`, and that exactly **8 of 18** commands escalate on heavy — and you have on-disk evidence that yours did.

## 4 · Patch class vs full SDLC (15 min)

1. Small, surgical change: `Rename the Query button to Run. adw_patch_iso`. Run it.
2. Compare its footprint to the full SDLC runs: the plan lives under `specs/patch/`, and only a subset of phases ran (worktree → targeted patch plan → implement → PR — no full plan/test/review/document ceremony).

**Checkpoint 4 (one sentence):** why is the CSV/text-rename class routed to `patch` instead of `sdlc`? *(It's "just changing one thing" — a different composition of the same primitives, not a smaller SDLC. This is the secret in miniature.)*

## 5 · The ZTE ceremony and the uppercase interlock (30 min)

1. The low-stakes class ZTE is earned on first: `/chore adw_sdlc_ZTE_iso - change the background color to light green` (**ZTE uppercase**). Watch all six phases; note the initiation comment's auto-merge warning.
2. Confirm the merge landed **with no human step**: `git pull && git log --oneline -3` shows a merge commit `...via ADW Ship workflow`, and the app served from `main` shows the change.
3. **Prove the interlock is real, not a bug:** repeat with lowercase `adw_sdlc_zte_iso`. `/classify_adw` downgrades it to reviewed `adw_sdlc_iso` — no auto-merge. Typing lowercase and mistaking the working safety for a defect is the trap; you just disarmed it.

**Checkpoint 5:** you can list the ship gates in order (8 non-null state fields → three-way worktree validation → fetch/checkout main/pull/`merge --no-ff`/push, original branch restored on any failure) and explain what a lowercase `zte` does and why.

## 6 · Read the KPIs, close the loop, clean up (15 min)

1. Open `app_docs/agentic_kpis.md` after your runs. Recompute **attempts** (count entries in `all_adws`) and **streak** by hand and confirm the table agrees — the KPI command forces its arithmetic through `python -c` for exactly this reason.
2. **Clean up every run:** `./scripts/purge_tree.sh <adw_id>` for each; confirm `git worktree list` is clean. Each worktree is a full repo copy — skipping this is how a laptop fills up silently.

**Checkpoint 6:** your KPI table matches your hand-computed numbers, your worktrees are purged, and you can state the presence you recorded for each run in sections 1, 3, 4, and 5 — and which class you'd trust to ZTE next.

## 7 · Stretch drills (30–60 min, still zero hand-typing)

**S1 — Parallel proof.** File two independent chores; launch both ADWs back to back. Capture `git worktree list` and both `.ports.env` while both run; explain in one line why they can't collide (disjoint worktree, branch, ports, state).
**S2 — Port-collision drill.** Occupy a run's deterministic port (`python3 -m http.server 9107`) before launching; find in the logs where `find_next_available_ports` picked the fallback slot.
**S3 — Break ship's gate on purpose.** Run `adw_plan_iso` only, then `adw_ship_iso <issue#> <adw-id>`. Screenshot the missing-fields abort and read off exactly which of the eight required state fields were null.
**S4 — Docs-vs-code audit (technical-accuracy drill).** `adws/README.md` says ship "approves the PR and merges using squash." Read `adw_ship_iso.py` and write three sentences on what it *actually* does (`--no-ff` manual merge + push) and how `git log` would reveal the difference. Dan's narration matches the code, not the README.
**S5 — Latent-bug hunt.** Find the bug in `worktree_ops.remove_worktree` (a `shutil.rmtree` fallback with no `import shutil`); explain the failure path and why `validate_worktree`'s three-way check still keeps pipelines honest.
**S6 — E2E honesty check.** Prove from `adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py` that E2E is *always* skipped in SDLC/ZTE (`--skip-e2e`), then run it yourself standalone: `uv run adw_test_iso.py <issue#> <adw-id>` (no `--skip-e2e`) with Playwright MCP configured.
**S7 — Extend the review one step toward video.** `playwright-mcp-config.json` already records 1920×1080 video per run. After a review, locate the worktree's `videos/` dir and post the file path (or an uploaded URL) back to the issue — Dan's named "next most obvious" review upgrade.
**S8 — Wire the real trigger.** `uv run adw_triggers/trigger_webhook.py` (port 8001) + `scripts/expose_webhook.sh` (cloudflared) + a repo webhook for Issues/Issue-comments. File an issue from your phone and stay fully out of the loop. (Cron alternative: `trigger_cron.py`, 20s poll.)

## Pass criteria (all required)

- [ ] One `adw_sdlc_iso` run completed end-to-end from a GitHub issue with **zero hand-typed application code**; the issue thread shows classification, state, plan, test, review (with ≥1 screenshot), documentation, and a PR
- [ ] You can print `agents/<adw_id>/adw_state.json` and explain every one of the ten fields, including why ship refuses to run when any of its **eight** required fields is null
- [ ] A `model_set heavy` run demonstrably used **opus** for implementation while classification stayed on **sonnet** (evidence from `raw_output.jsonl` or the issue thread)
- [ ] One **uppercase-ZTE** run merged to main with a `via ADW Ship workflow` merge commit, AND one lowercase `zte` attempt visibly downgraded to reviewed SDLC
- [ ] Two ADW runs executed concurrently with disjoint worktrees, branches, and port pairs (evidence captured)
- [ ] `app_docs/agentic_kpis.md` was updated by your runs; you recomputed attempts/streak/presence by hand and they match
- [ ] All worktrees purged; `git worktree list` clean
- [ ] Oral, no notes: Tactic #7; the three velocity-scale levels with presence numbers; PITER→PITE; the secret (composable agentic primitives, SDLC = one arrangement)

## Instructor / self-check notes

- **The presence log is the real assessment.** A student who reaches ZTE on the background-color chore but records honest presence for the heavier runs has learned the lesson. A student who declares "ZTE for everything" has missed it entirely — push back exactly as Dan does at 32:37.
- **Most common stall:** running a dependent workflow (`adw_ship_iso`, `adw_review_iso`, `adw_document_iso`) without the adw-id — they hard-exit demanding prior state. The ADW ID chains everything; copy it from the plan-phase output.
- **"It merged to main automatically — is that safe?"** That IS ZTE, and it's gated (8-field state validation → three-way worktree check → `--no-ff` merge with branch-restore on failure → the uppercase interlock upstream). Have them count the gates before they panic; then discuss the *deterministic* controls current practice layers on top (branch protection, required CI, merge queues) — see `outdated-vs-current.md`.
- **"My screenshots are just file paths — it's broken."** No — the R2 uploader disables silently without the four Cloudflare vars. Local paths are the expected fallback.
- **Cost anxiety is on-schedule.** This lab spends the most in the course (opus on heavy + a full ship). If the budget conversation surfaces, that's the pay-to-play lesson arriving; ZTE's whole premise is that the layer's compounding payoff justifies the spend.
- **Don't let doc-blaming slide into tool-blaming.** S4 (README says squash, code does `--no-ff`) is the transferable habit: verify countable claims against source. Reward the student who reads `adw_ship_iso.py` over the one who quotes the README.
- **What this lab intentionally does NOT cover:** production-grade deploy guardrails (canaries, deterministic deploy checks, three-agent review fusion), the full fleet-of-agents endgame, and lesson 8's atoms-and-compositions map. Keep ZTE framed as a *targeted* North Star per problem class and deflect scope creep into "delete your whole SDLC."
# TAC-7 — Outdated vs Current (course era → mid-2026)

TAC-7 ages unusually well because its payload is a *North Star and a set of primitives*, not a toolchain: the velocity scale (in-loop → out-loop → ZTE), the presence KPI, and "the secret is composable agentic primitives, not the SDLC" are timeless, and Dan pre-empts the churn on camera ("I don't care how you scale up your agent environments, it only matters that you can"). But it's the Advanced, second-to-last lesson, wired to a specific mid-2025 tooling moment, and six things have moved. For each: what the course shows, what current practice is, and whether the book needs an overlay. Every gap below narrows the *plumbing*; none touches the doctrine.

---

## 1. Hand-rolled worktree orchestration → isolation as a first-class platform feature  ⟵ the big named gap

**Course era:** parallel isolation is built from raw materials — `git worktree add -b` from `origin/main`, base-36-hash port arithmetic (9100–9114 / 9200–9214), an agent-run `/install_worktree` prompt, and `scripts/purge_tree.sh` to reclaim the full-repo copies. Pedagogically perfect: every moving part is visible.
**Current:** Claude Code (mid-2026) exposes the same tactic natively — worktree-isolated subagents/tasks, sandboxed bash, and cloud/remote agent execution; multi-agent fan-out over isolated environments is a standard orchestration pattern rather than custom scripting. The CONCEPT — isolated, parallel, disposable agent environments with per-run state — transfers unchanged; the code you'd write today is smaller.
**Book treatment:** overlay REQUIRED. Teach the hand-rolled worktree version first (you see the ports, the state, the install), then show the same isolation as native worktree-isolated agents.
**Overlay:** `fop_yxV-mPo` — *Codebase Singularity* (include_in_phase1: **yes**) — TAC-7 is the lesson where "the codebase runs itself" stops being a vision and becomes a demo; this video develops that self-operating end-state. (Deferred out of TAC-1 explicitly *to* this lesson.)

## 2. `subprocess claude -p` pipelines → the Claude Agent SDK

**Course era:** `adws/adw_modules/agent.py` is a subprocess wrapper — `claude -p --model X --output-format stream-json --verbose --dangerously-skip-permissions`, JSONL parsing, a `[1,3,5]s` retry ladder, per-agent session capture.
**Current:** that layer is exactly what the Claude Agent SDK (Python/TS) now provides first-class — structured outputs, hooks, session management, and error semantics. The ADW architecture maps 1:1; a 2026 rebuild swaps `agent.py` for the SDK and keeps every workflow script's shape.
**Book treatment:** margin note on the `agent.py` walkthrough — "this is the SDK, hand-rolled"; teach the ADW composition idea as durable, the subprocess plumbing as period detail.
**Overlay:** `6fCqj4xFCZI` — *How Claude Code CHANGED Engineering Forever* (include_in_phase1: **yes**) — bridges the ADW plumbing the student just read to how the same embedding is done today.

## 3. Reusable engineering as `.claude/commands` prompt files → Agent Skills

**Course era:** engineering is templated into 33 command prompts discovered by convention, plus the `conditional_docs.md` routing table — which is essentially hand-rolled skill discovery / progressive disclosure ("this lets our agents scale with the size of our codebase").
**Current:** Agent Skills (`SKILL.md` with frontmatter, on-demand loading, composability) are the first-class packaging for the same idea; the conditional-docs pattern is precisely what Skills' progressive disclosure does natively.
**Book treatment:** overlay for the "template your engineering" section; teach the committed-prompt encoding, then show its current form as a Skill.
**Overlay:** `X2ciJedw2vU` — *RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL* (include_in_phase1: **yes**) — the committed one-purpose prompt becomes a `SKILL.md`.

## 4. Model names and model-set economics are period detail

**Course era:** `SLASH_COMMAND_MODEL_MAP` hardcodes `sonnet` / `opus` aliases circa mid-2025, and the demo app bumps `gpt-4.1` → `o4-mini` as its worked chore.
**Current:** model families have turned over since (Claude 4-class → Claude 5 era); the specific ids are dated the day they ship. The durable idea is the **base/heavy KNOB** — per-command compute allocation via an IDK stored in state — not any model name. Update the map's *values*, keep its *shape*.
**Book treatment:** "period detail" callout on the model-map frame (t1010); teach the knob and the 8-of-18 selectivity as the transferable lesson.
**Overlay:** none required.

## 5. ZTE guardrails have matured beyond an uppercase check

**Course era:** the auto-merge safety is charming prompt-level engineering — `/classify_adw` refuses `adw_sdlc_ZTE_iso` unless "ZTE" is literally uppercase — plus the pipeline's own gates (test/review abort, 8-field state validation, three-way worktree check, `--no-ff` merge with branch-restore).
**Current:** production practice layers *deterministic* controls around auto-merge: branch protection + required CI checks, permission modes and hooks gating tool use, environment-scoped credentials, staged deploys / canaries, and merge queues. Dan hints at exactly this evolution in-lesson — "add that key deploy check step, a bunch of deterministic deploy checks... parallelize the review step so three agents have to agree — run the fusion chain on agents."
**Book treatment:** teach the in-repo gates as the *concept* (a pipeline that refuses to merge in N places), then a "current practice" callout naming the deterministic controls a real deploy would add. Keep the uppercase interlock as a teaching artifact of prompt-level safety, not a recommendation.
**Overlay:** none required (Dan's own "deterministic deploy checks / three-agent fusion" line carries it).

## 6. Review evidence: screenshots → video and richer review products

**Course era:** the review step's proof is 1–5 numbered screenshots uploaded to R2 and posted to the issue.
**Current:** the lesson names its own next step — "the next most obvious one to build out is video. Have your agents take video, upload it to a cloud bucket, and post the URLs." The repo's `playwright-mcp-config.json` *already records* 1920×1080 video per run, and Dan calls comprehensive review systems "an entire product category that is important to build out" — which is precisely what agent-review tooling became.
**Book treatment:** footnote the video path as the intended evolution (the config is already there — see lab stretch S7); keep screenshots as the worked example. The principle — proof lands IN the review system, zero clicks to verify — transfers unchanged.
**Overlay:** none required.

---

## What has NOT aged (teach with full confidence)

- The **Agentic Engineering Velocity Scale** — in-loop → out-loop → ZTE — and that it's a per-problem-class gradient, not a switch.
- **Presence** as the KPI under the whole course arc: TAC-1 removed typing, TAC-7 removes reviewing, presence tracks both.
- **The secret: composable agentic primitives**, with the SDLC demoted to one arbitrary arrangement ("a package to deliver a message"). This is the course's thesis and has no expiry.
- **Review = proof of value** ("is what we built what we asked for?", proved), distinct from testing; blockers-only resolution.
- The **ADW state object** as pipeline memory outside the context window — the thing that lets single-purpose agents chain.
- **Isolation, parallelism, disposability** as the requirement (worktrees are one implementation of it).
- The **base/heavy compute knob** — encode the allocation once, invoke with one word.
- **Evidence-gated, per-class** decisions to drop review — KPIs, not vibes; "how else will you know unless you're trying?"
- **"Target" ZTE**: chores → bugs → features; a North Star you earn, not a day-one state.
- The **belief section** — "if you don't believe your agents can run your codebase, you're cooked"; "prepared to win, not right."
- **Verify countable claims against source, not docs** — the README-says-squash / code-does-`--no-ff` drift is a permanent teaching artifact, not a dated feature.

