+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="mastery">
+        <span class="section-number">14</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Cumulative mastery · retrieve, trace, decide, compose</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Close the chapter. Answer aloud or on paper. Then reveal. Mastery means you can predict source behavior, distinguish the video from the snapshot, and choose a workflow shape—not merely recognize the vocabulary.</p>
+
+        <h3>Round 1 · Velocity and presence</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative velocity quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Give the three velocity levels and the human-presence pattern at each.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>In-loop has continuous human presence; out-loop’s best case is two touches, prompt and review/merge; ZTE targets one touch, the prompt.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is the scale a gradient rather than a switch?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Movement is per problem class, based on evidence, and reversible. Dan himself returns in-loop for hands-on review at 22:59.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What does “Target” prevent?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It prevents interpreting ZTE as a global day-one auto-merge policy. It is a North Star pursued chores → bugs → features as each class earns trust.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What exactly changes from PITER to PITE?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The human R/review touchpoint drops. Evaluation remains embedded in tests, agentic review, proof, resolution, state validation, and ship gates.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 2 · Trace one ADW</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative ADW trace quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. A new issue triggers an isolated run. Name the four resources that must be unique.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Worktree/path, Git branch, backend/frontend port pair, and state/log directory keyed by ADW ID.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. List all ten state fields and identify ship’s eight.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Ten: ADW ID, issue number, branch name, plan file, issue class, worktree path, backend port, frontend port, model set, and all ADWs. Ship requires the first eight; model set and all ADWs are not in its completeness set.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. How are the first ports derived, and what happens on collision?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The first eight alphanumeric ID characters are interpreted base 36, modulo 15, mapping to 9100–9114 and 9200–9214. Both ports are socket-checked; the function advances through the 15 pairs until one is free.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Why does state belong outside the context window?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It lets fresh, single-purpose agents coordinate and replay phases without sharing a giant transcript or making the human carry pipeline memory.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 3 · Compute and evidence</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative compute and review quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What does <code>model_set heavy</code> actually select?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The heavy column of an 18-command policy map. Eight commands escalate in the course source; ten stay on the base model.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. How do testing and review ask different questions?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Testing evaluates executable contracts and whether code works. Review compares the built result with what was requested and produces proof of value.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Which review severity blocks, and why not block all findings?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Only <code>blocker</code>. Treating skippable or tech-debt observations as release failures makes review cry wolf and prevents useful autonomous progress.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What proves that a human review may be a bottleneck?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A class-level history where attempts stay low, streak rises, evidence remains correct, blockers are absent, and human reviews repeatedly add no findings while presence remains two.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 4 · Predict the ZTE source</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative ZTE control flow quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. A learner types lowercase <code>adw_sdlc_zte_iso</code>. What happens?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>/classify_adw</code> refuses the dangerous ZTE route and downgrades to reviewed <code>adw_sdlc_iso</code>.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Tests pass, review passes, documentation fails. Does ship run?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Yes. The orchestrator warns that documentation failed but explicitly continues to shipping.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. State is complete, but the worktree directory was deleted. Does merge begin?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. Three-way worktree validation fails because the filesystem path does not exist.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Give the real Git order and merge strategy.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Save original branch; fetch origin; checkout main; pull origin/main; merge the feature branch with <code>--no-ff</code> and the ADW ship message; push origin/main; restore the original branch. Failure paths attempt restoration.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">5. What important test did the ZTE orchestrator not run?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Browser E2E. It always passes <code>--skip-e2e</code>; standalone <code>adw_test_iso.py</code> without the flag is required.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <h3>Round 5 · Compose and teach</h3>
+        <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Cumulative composition quiz">
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. State the secret and explain why it does not invalidate the course.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The subject is composable agentic primitives for any engineering problem class; the SDLC is one arrangement. The course used the SDLC as a package for teaching the primitives and their interactions.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Compose a narrow patch from available steps.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Issue/trigger → isolated worktree and state → targeted patch plan → focused implementation → PR. The exact course patch path omits full test/review/document ceremony.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Name the three 2026 plumbing shifts overlaid in this chapter.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Hand-rolled worktree fan-out toward first-class isolated tasks; <code>claude -p</code> subprocess orchestration toward Agent SDKs; command Markdown/conditional docs toward Agent Skills.</p></details></div>
+          <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What is your next bounded experiment?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A passing answer names one course-code problem class, its route, required evidence, current and target presence, and an abort/reversal condition.</p></details></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <div class="paradox">
+          <blockquote>The highest-leverage result is not one autonomous change. It is a codebase with primitives you can keep recomposing as models, tools, and problem classes change.</blockquote>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="glossary">
+        <span class="section-number">15</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">TAC-7 glossary</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Use these spellings when teaching or debugging. The chapter preserves the course’s intentional ZTE naming duality and separates runtime demo values from committed evidence.</p>
+        <dl class="glossary">
+          <div><dt>ADW</dt><dd>AI Developer Workflow: the scripting-layer workflow that chains focused agents and engineering phases for a problem class.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Agentic Engineering Velocity Scale</dt><dd>In-Loop → Out-Loop → ZTE, a per-class gradient in required human presence.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>ADW state object</dt><dd><code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/adw_state.json</code>, the ten-field per-run pipeline memory and part of the ship gate.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>all_adws</dt><dd>The state list of workflow identifiers that have run; used by KPI tracking to reason about attempts.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Composable agentic primitives</dt><dd>Reusable ADWs, templates, plans, architecture, tests, docs, types, stdout, tools, prompts, models, and context from which workflows are built.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Evidence surface</dt><dd>The place a claim can be checked: agent logs/state, isolated worktree, issue comments/screenshots, PR, test output, or Git history.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>IDK</dt><dd>Information-dense keyword whose committed interpretation expands into a larger instruction or policy, such as <code>model_set heavy</code>.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>In-Loop</dt><dd>Velocity level with continuous human steering, inspection, or transition work.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Isolated ADW / <code>_iso</code></dt><dd>A workflow running in its own course worktree with dedicated branch, ports, and state.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Model set</dt><dd>A named base/heavy per-command compute-allocation map stored in ADW state.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Out-Loop</dt><dd>Velocity level where the human prompts, leaves, and returns to review/merge; best-case presence two.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>PITER → PITE</dt><dd>Prompt input, Trigger, Environment, Review becomes Prompt input, Trigger, Environment as the human review touch drops.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Presence</dt><dd>KPI counting human touchpoints per shipped unit; ZTE targets one.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Proof of value</dt><dd>Review evidence answering “Is what we built what we asked for?” In TAC-7 this includes numbered screenshots posted to the issue.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Streak</dt><dd>Consecutive successful run count used with presence, attempts, and size to judge per-class readiness.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Target Zero-Touch Engineering</dt><dd>Tactic #7: the North Star of reducing human presence from two to one for a proven problem class.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Zero Touch Execution</dt><dd>The expansion used by the course code and bot messages for the ZTE workflow that appends ship to the full SDLC.</dd></div>
+          <div><dt>Worktree validation</dt><dd>The three-way check that state names a path, the directory exists, and Git registers the worktree.</dd></div>
+        </dl>
+      </section>
+
+      <footer class="chapter-footer">
+        <p class="fine">Built from the TAC-7 extract, transcript-grounded frames, and the distributed course codebase. Video runtime issue IDs are illustrative; code walkthroughs use committed snapshot evidence.</p>
+        <div class="chapter-footer__nav">
+          <a href="06-let-your-agents-focus.html">← Chapter 06 · Let Your Agents Focus</a>
+          <a href="../index.html">Book hub →</a>
+        </div>
+      </footer>
+    </main>
+  </div>
+
+  <script>
+    (() => {
+      const topBar = document.getElementById('top-progress');
+      const railBar = document.getElementById('rail-progress');
+      const label = document.getElementById('progress-label');
+      const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.chapter-nav a')];
+      const sections = links
+        .map(link => document.querySelector(link.getAttribute('href')))
+        .filter(Boolean);
+      let progressFramePending = false;
+
+      const updateProgress = () => {
+        const root = document.documentElement;
+        const distance = root.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
+        const progress = distance > 0 ? Math.min(1, Math.max(0, window.scrollY / distance)) : 0;
+        const value = `${Math.round(progress * 100)}%`;
+        topBar.style.width = value;
+        railBar.style.width = value;
+        label.textContent = value;
+        progressFramePending = false;
+      };
+
+      const requestProgressUpdate = () => {
+        if (progressFramePending) return;
+        progressFramePending = true;
+        requestAnimationFrame(updateProgress);
+      };
+
+      const observer = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
+        const visible = entries
+          .filter(entry => entry.isIntersecting)
+          .sort((a, b) => b.intersectionRatio - a.intersectionRatio)[0];
+        if (!visible) return;
+        links.forEach(link => {
+          const active = link.getAttribute('href') === `#${visible.target.id}`;
+          if (active) link.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location');
+          else link.removeAttribute('aria-current');
+        });
+      }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -65% 0px', threshold: [0, .1, .5] });
+
+      sections.forEach(section => observer.observe(section));
+      updateProgress();
+      window.addEventListener('scroll', requestProgressUpdate, { passive: true });
+      window.addEventListener('resize', requestProgressUpdate);
+    })();
+  </script>
+</body>
+</html>

