+        </details>
+      </div>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section" id="orientation">
+        <span class="section-number">00</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">First earn the right to remove yourself</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">“Automatically merge agent work to main” is either the culmination of seven lessons or an irresponsible slogan. The difference is whether you can point to the isolation, persistent state, tests, review evidence, resolution loop, shipping validation, and per-class performance history that make the decision inspectable.</p>
+
+        <p>TAC-7 is deliberately shaped unlike a conventional lecture. For roughly the first half, Dan does not announce the tactic. He collects dividends. Five pieces of work enter the agentic layer, take different workflow routes, run in parallel, and return proofs and pull requests while he reviews. Only after that machinery is visible does he name the velocity scale, identify the human review as the new bottleneck, launch a zero-touch chore, and reveal the course’s secret. The teaching order matters: <strong>evidence before doctrine</strong>.</p>
+
+        <p>The committed repository and the on-video run are related but not identical snapshots. The video shows runtime issues #51, #52, #54, #62, a random-data feature, and a CSV-text patch. Those issue numbers and run IDs do not live in the distributed snapshot. The repository instead preserves issues 3, 9, 15, and 47 as five application documents, one specification, agent run artifacts, and a KPI record for ADW <code>cc73faf1</code>. We will use the video values to tell the lesson’s story and the committed values when we walk code. That separation keeps the chapter honest.</p>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--tactic">
+          <p class="callout__label">The chapter’s one-sentence thesis</p>
+          <p>Target a presence of one only for problem classes whose isolated, observable, closed-loop workflow has repeatedly proved that your review no longer adds value; then remember that the workflow itself is one composition of reusable agentic primitives.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <h3>What you are training</h3>
+        <ul class="learning-objectives">
+          <li><strong>Calibration</strong>Choose in-loop, out-loop, or ZTE per problem class and defend the choice with presence and streak evidence.</li>
+          <li><strong>Tracing</strong>Follow one ADW through issue, worktree, ports, state, agent logs, tests, screenshots, documentation, PR, and ship.</li>
+          <li><strong>Source audit</strong>Verify countable claims in the Python source when the README and the code disagree.</li>
+          <li><strong>Composition</strong>Separate agentic primitives from ADW phases and assemble the workflow shape appropriate to the problem.</li>
+        </ul>
+
+        <h3>The chapter contract</h3>
+        <p>Every major idea uses the same learning cycle: Hook → Picture → Story → Name → Worked example → You try → Fail/fix → Current Practice → Retrieval → Teach-back. Do not skip the “You try” moves merely because this is an advanced chapter. TAC-7’s most dangerous misunderstandings are verbal: a learner can repeat “zero touch” while assuming browser E2E ran, confusing review with tests, trusting stale merge documentation, or turning a per-class target into a global policy.</p>
+
+        <div class="callout callout--warning">
+          <p class="callout__label">Prime directive for the chapter and lab</p>
+          <p>Type zero lines of application code by hand. Record every human touch honestly. Prompt only is presence 1; prompt plus review is 2; continuous steering is in-loop. A higher number is not a moral failure. A dishonest number is.</p>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-payday">
+        <span class="section-number">01</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Payday: five pieces of work, one engineer, no application-code typing</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The first lesson is not “go autonomous.” It is that a finished agentic layer converts short issue prompts into concurrent, production-shaped work while the engineer spends attention elsewhere.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>Your queue is serial because you are the transition</h3>
+          <p>Imagine three features, one chore, and one bug arrive before lunch. Even if a coding agent can produce each change, you still open the ticket, restate it, choose a branch, wait, launch tests, inspect the result, ask for repair, and prepare the pull request. Five “AI-assisted” tasks remain serial because every task must pass through your attention between phases. The bottleneck is no longer typing. It is transition management.</p>
+          <p>Dan’s counterexample is intentionally operational. He writes five GitHub issues in about six minutes. The issues are not ornate specifications. They combine plain-language intent with information-dense routing keywords. A webhook sees each event, classifies it, creates an isolated environment, and starts the chosen workflow. While those runs proceed, Dan turns to review. The system, not the human, carries the transitions.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — one issue becomes a pipeline input</p>
+          <h3>Intent plus two routing signals</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t0250-issue-as-prompt.png" alt="GitHub new issue form used as the prompt surface for an isolated feature workflow" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">4:10</span>The issue is both request and machine input. Plain English supplies the work; <code>/feature</code> and <code>adw_sdlc_iso</code> classify and route it.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <div class="workflow workflow--spaced" aria-label="Issue to concurrent workflow">
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Issue</strong><code>intent + class</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Trigger</strong><code>webhook event</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Environment</strong><code>tree + ports</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Workflow</strong><code>patch or SDLC</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Evidence</strong><code>issue + PR</code></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — the fan-out begins</p>
+          <h3>The terminal sees five events, not one overloaded agent</h3>
+          <p>The video’s work queue is concrete: issue #51 expands a drop zone; #52 adds JSON export; another feature generates random table data and asks for <code>model_set heavy</code>; #54 changes the NL-to-SQL model to o4-mini; and a CSV-text defect takes a targeted patch path. At 5:22 Dan explicitly chooses the lightweight patch workflow for that narrow bug instead of sending every item through the full SDLC. The secret is already present in miniature: different problem classes receive different compositions.</p>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t0372-webhook-events-terminal.png" alt="Webhook terminal receiving issue events and initializing isolated agent workflows" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">6:12</span>The trigger detects events and initializes runs. The visible 9106/9206 pair belongs to this on-video run; each learner’s ports depend on the ADW ID.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>By 7:08, the lesson can call the result “absurd levels of scale” without hand-waving. Five issues are moving at once. That does not mean one context window is juggling five tasks. It means five focused workflows have independent state and environments. Concurrency is a property of the layer.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>Collecting the dividends of the agentic layer</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s framing</p>
+            <p><strong>Collecting dividends:</strong> receiving repeated output from the planning, loop, focus, trigger, and environment investments made in earlier lessons. “Once you’ve built the system that builds the system, it’s going to pay you back massively.”</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>In plain English, there is no new trick in the five-issue demo. TAC-4 supplied PITER and AFK execution. TAC-5 supplied validation and resolution loops. TAC-6 supplied one-agent-one-prompt-one-purpose. TAC-7 composes those capabilities, runs them in parallel, and asks whether the final human review can be removed for a proven class of work.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — route the five jobs</p>
+          <h3>Workflow choice is already an engineering decision</h3>
+          <table>
+            <thead><tr><th>Video work item</th><th>Problem class</th><th>Routing detail</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
+            <tbody>
+              <tr><td>Drop-zone surface</td><td>Feature</td><td><code>adw_sdlc_iso</code></td><td>Needs the full evidence-producing chain.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>JSON export</td><td>Feature</td><td><code>adw_sdlc_iso</code></td><td>Runs independently in its own environment.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Random table data</td><td>Feature</td><td><code>model_set heavy</code></td><td>Allocates more compute selectively.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Model bump</td><td>Chore</td><td>SDLC review loop</td><td>Still returns proof and a PR.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>CSV-text fix</td><td>Narrow bug</td><td><code>adw_patch_iso</code></td><td>A different composition, not a miniature SDLC.</td></tr>
+            </tbody>
+          </table>
+          <p>The table prevents a common abstraction leak. “Parallel agents” is not the lesson’s unit. The unit is a <em>routed workflow for a problem class</em>, with one focused agent at each phase and durable evidence between phases.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — classify before you automate</p>
+          <h3>Make a five-card routing board</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Write the five video work items on cards.</li>
+            <li>For each, choose <code>patch</code>, reviewed <code>sdlc</code>, or a future ZTE target.</li>
+            <li>State what evidence the run must return before you would review less.</li>
+            <li>Circle which two could safely execute concurrently and name the isolation each needs.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass when your choices differ by problem class. If every card receives the same maximum ceremony, you have copied a pipeline rather than learned to compose one.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: searching for lesson 7’s new magic component</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you scan <code>adws/</code> for a novel multi-agent framework and dismiss the demo when you find ordinary Python orchestration, subprocesses, Git worktrees, and saved JSON.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> identify the composition delta. The machinery is intentionally familiar. The capability comes from putting triggers, isolated environments, persistent state, focused phase agents, validation loops, review evidence, and workflow-specific routing behind one issue-shaped input. The lesson’s new move is scale plus removal of the last human—not a new primitive.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>Modern platforms can shrink the plumbing</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p>The course makes every trigger, worktree, port, subprocess, and state transition visible. Newer agent platforms can provide isolation and fan-out as first-class features. Keep the architectural questions visible even when the setup code becomes smaller: what is the unit of work, what owns state, what prevents collision, and where does evidence land?</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">The chapter’s first public-video overlay appears with the isolation idea, where the plumbing change is concrete.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Collecting dividends retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Which of the five work items did not receive the full SDLC?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The CSV-text bug. It was routed to <code>adw_patch_iso</code>, using a targeted patch plan, implementation, and PR.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What is actually new in the TAC-7 demo?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Not a new primitive: earlier tactics are composed, executed concurrently, and then used to justify removing the final human review for a proven problem class.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why are the on-video issue numbers not lab pass criteria?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>They are runtime values from Dan’s demonstration, not artifacts in the distributed snapshot. Learners create issues in repositories they control and receive different numbers and ADW IDs.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Teach a teammate why five simultaneous coding chats are not equivalent to the five-issue demo.</strong> Your explanation must mention routing, isolated environments, focused phases, persistent evidence, and why the patch job should not be forced through the full SDLC.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if the listener could sketch the layer that carries transitions without the engineer.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-scale">
+        <span class="section-number">02</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The Agentic Engineering Velocity Scale is a gradient</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The most useful question in the chapter is not “Are you autonomous?” It is “How present are you for this class of work, and what evidence would let you move one step?”</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>The false binary produces both fear and recklessness</h3>
+          <p>If agentic work is framed as human-in-the-loop versus fully autonomous, teams are pushed toward two bad choices. One group remains continuously present because anything else sounds unsafe. Another declares autonomy as a global mode and removes review before the system can prove itself. Both miss the intermediate discipline that the course has been building.</p>
+          <p>Dan rejects the binary directly: it is “in loop moving toward out loop.” The verb matters. You move as repeatable work earns trust. You can also move backward. At 22:59, Dan deliberately uses <code>/in_loop_review</code> to inspect the drag-and-drop branch himself. That is not a contradiction. It is an expert selecting the right position on the scale.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — three levels, one measurable dimension</p>
+          <h3>Presence falls as the layer carries more of the chain</h3>
+          <div class="velocity-scale" role="img" aria-label="Velocity scale from continuous human presence through two touches to one touch">
+            <article class="velocity-scale__step"><strong>1 · In-Loop</strong><span>Continuous presence</span><p>You steer, inspect, and continue the agent turn by turn.</p></article>
+            <article class="velocity-scale__step"><strong>2 · Out-Loop</strong><span>Presence ≈ 2</span><p>You provide the prompt, then return to review and merge.</p></article>
+            <article class="velocity-scale__step velocity-scale__step--target"><strong>3 · ZTE</strong><span>Presence = 1</span><p>You provide the prompt; the gated pipeline ships without another touch.</p></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>The numbers 1, 2, and 3 name levels on the course card. Presence is a separate KPI. In-loop presence is continuous rather than a fixed count; out-loop’s best case is two touchpoints; ZTE targets one.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — the lesson traverses the scale</p>
+          <h3>One video, three operating modes</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t0100-velocity-scale.jpg" alt="The Agentic Engineering Velocity Scale showing in-loop, out-loop, and ZTE" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">1:40</span>The lesson names the three levels before traversing all of them. The scale is not a global mode selector; every problem class earns its own position.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The lesson begins with the familiar interactive <code>claude</code> setup and installation ritual: in-loop work. The five-issue demo is out-loop: Dan writes issue prompts, lets the workflows run, and returns to proof and pull requests. Issue #62 supplies ZTE: one chore prompt enters, all phases run, and main receives the merged change. Between those beats, Dan chooses to step back in-loop for a hands-on browser review.</p>
+          <p>This sequence is stronger than a definition because it shows that the same engineer can occupy different levels in one hour. The scale belongs to work, risk, evidence, and system maturity—not to personal identity. You are not “a ZTE engineer” in a way that forbids review. You are an engineer who can explain why a specific class is currently at presence one, two, or continuous.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>Agentic Engineering Velocity Scale</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Canonical scale</p>
+            <p><strong>In-Loop → Out-Loop → ZTE.</strong> A per-problem-class progression in how much human presence an engineering workflow requires.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>Velocity here is not raw generation speed. It is the speed at which valuable work can traverse a trustworthy system without waiting for human transitions. A fast in-loop model may feel responsive while still limiting throughput to one engineer’s attention. A slower out-loop workflow can create more organizational velocity if it carries several jobs and returns evidence asynchronously.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — score three classes</p>
+          <h3>The same repository can live at three levels</h3>
+          <table class="presence-ledger">
+            <thead><tr><th>Problem class</th><th>Current level</th><th>Presence</th><th>Evidence for the next move</th></tr></thead>
+            <tbody>
+              <tr><td>Unfamiliar feature</td><td>In-loop or out-loop</td><td>Continuous or 2</td><td>Stable plan, tests, proof screenshots, no blocker repairs.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Known narrow patch</td><td>Out-loop</td><td>2</td><td>Repeated correct routing and clean diffs with review adding no findings.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Low-stakes styling chore</td><td>ZTE target</td><td>1</td><td>Passing pipeline, review proof, positive streak, and reliable ship gates.</td></tr>
+            </tbody>
+          </table>
+          <p>This is an interpretation exercise, not a universal policy. Dan proposes the maturity order chores → bugs → features. The background-color example is a chore precisely because it offers low-hanging fruit on which to test the final step.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — make presence visible</p>
+          <h3>Start a four-run presence ledger</h3>
+          <p>Draw four rows for the capstone runs: reviewed SDLC, heavy SDLC, patch, and uppercase ZTE. Before you execute, predict a presence number. Afterward, record the real number and every touch that produced it. If you opened logs or the issue thread only to observe, count according to the chapter’s operational rule: a human decision or intervention is a touch; passive system observation during training is evidence collection, not a hidden production approval.</p>
+          <p>Then answer: for which class did your manual review change the outcome? The question turns “confidence” into an empirical decision.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: turning the scale into a maturity badge</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you feel embarrassed to move a workflow back in-loop, or you report presence 1 even though you manually checked and approved the merge.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> score the run, not yourself. Movement backward can be the correct response to a new problem class, changed code, weak evidence, or an unexpected failure. Honest presence data is the instrument that eventually lets you remove review. Inflated data destroys the basis for the decision.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>The scale survives every tool generation</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Durable practice</p>
+            <p>Whether a modern platform calls the work a background task, remote agent, isolated subagent, or workflow run, the calibration question remains: how many human touchpoints does a shipped unit require? Record the answer per class, and distinguish observation from approval.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">The course’s specific models and orchestration code will age; the presence gradient does not.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Velocity scale retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the three levels and their presence shape.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>In-loop: continuous presence. Out-loop: best-case presence 2, prompt then review/merge. ZTE: presence 1, prompt only.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What did Dan do at 22:59 that disproves a one-way global mode switch?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>He deliberately ran <code>/in_loop_review</code> and inspected a feature himself, moving back in-loop for that work.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What class order does Dan recommend for targeting ZTE?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Chores first, then bugs, then features.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Explain the scale without using “manual” or “autonomous.”</strong> Use human touchpoints, problem classes, evidence, and Dan’s deliberate return to in-loop review. End with one condition that would make you move a ZTE-targeted class backward.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if the explanation makes room for reversible, evidence-based movement.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-isolation">
+        <span class="section-number">03</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Parallel scale requires isolated, disposable environments</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Five workflows cannot safely share one branch, one checkout, one frontend port, and one backend port. TAC-7 makes the environment boundary explicit: one ADW ID owns one worktree, one branch, one state record, and one available port pair.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>Two correct agents can still corrupt each other</h3>
+          <p>Agent A checks out a feature branch while Agent B believes it is testing a bug branch. Both write into the same directory. Both start the frontend on 5173. One resets the database while the other captures review evidence. Each agent may follow its prompt correctly and the combined system still fails. Intelligence does not compensate for a shared mutable environment.</p>
+          <p>The environment problem becomes unavoidable when fan-out becomes real. A demo with one agent can hide it. Five concurrent workflows cannot. Isolation is therefore not operational polish added after prompting; it is a prerequisite for trustworthy concurrency.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — one tree per run</p>
+          <h3>A complete application behind each eight-character ID</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t0680-worktrees-trees-dir.png" alt="Editor file tree showing many isolated application worktrees under the trees directory" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">11:20</span>Each directory under <code>trees/</code> is a full application checkout for one ADW. It is large by design and disposable by design.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <div class="workflow workflow--spaced" aria-label="Isolation dimensions owned by one ADW">
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>ADW ID</strong><code>8 chars</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Worktree</strong><code>trees/&lt;id&gt;</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Branch</strong><code>issue/adw scoped</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Ports</strong><code>910x / 920x</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>State</strong><code>agents/&lt;id&gt;</code></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — build the environment from source</p>
+          <h3><code>adw_plan_iso.py</code> creates the agent’s world</h3>
+          <p>The plan phase creates <code>trees/&lt;adw_id&gt;/</code> using <code>git worktree add -b &lt;branch&gt; ... origin/main</code>. Port allocation converts the first eight alphanumeric characters of the ADW ID from base 36, takes modulo 15, and maps the result to backend 9100–9114 and frontend 9200–9214. It socket-checks both ports and walks the 15-slot ring if the deterministic pair is occupied.</p>
+          <p>The workflow writes <code>.ports.env</code>, then executes <code>/install_worktree</code> inside the new checkout. That prompt copies environment files, appends the ports, copies MCP and Playwright configuration, rewrites paths, syncs Python and Bun dependencies, and resets the database. At 12:58 Dan opens <code>code trees/&lt;id&gt;</code> and visits the agent’s branch as a guest. The isolated environment is not a metaphor; it is inspectable on disk.</p>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t0535-adws-directory.png" alt="Course repository ADW directory containing isolated workflow scripts" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">8:55</span>The <code>adws/</code> directory is the scripting layer. The <code>_iso</code> suffix signals workflows that operate in isolated worktrees.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>Isolated ADWs</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">The suffix</p>
+            <p><strong><code>_iso</code>:</strong> an ADW runs inside its own environment—in this course, a Git worktree with dedicated ports and state.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>Worktrees are an implementation, not the doctrine. Dan names Docker and E2B as alternatives and says the exact mechanism does not matter; the requirement is the ability to scale agent environments. The transferable contract is stronger than “use worktrees”: environments must be isolated, reproducible enough to install and test, observable during the run, and disposable afterward.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — calculate and verify a port pair</p>
+          <h3>Hash gives index 7</h3>
+          <p>If an ADW ID maps to index 7, <code>get_ports_for_adw</code> assigns backend <code>9107</code> and frontend <code>9207</code>. The values are written to:</p>
+          <pre><code>BACKEND_PORT=9107
+FRONTEND_PORT=9207
+VITE_BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:9107</code></pre>
+          <p>The file is <code>trees/&lt;adw_id&gt;/.ports.env</code>, and the backend/frontend values are also persisted in <code>adw_state.json</code>. If either port is unavailable, <code>find_next_available_ports</code> advances to the next pair, wraps after 14, and raises only after trying all 15 pairs. Determinism gives a predictable first choice; availability checking keeps it honest.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — prove two runs cannot collide</p>
+          <h3>Inspect, do not assume</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Launch two independent course-repo chores.</li>
+            <li>Capture <code>git worktree list</code> while both run.</li>
+            <li>Open each <code>.ports.env</code> and compare branch, path, backend, and frontend.</li>
+            <li>Open each state file and verify that no path or port pair is shared.</li>
+            <li>After the run, purge both with <code>./scripts/purge_tree.sh &lt;adw_id&gt;</code>.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass with four pieces of evidence: distinct worktree, branch, port pair, and state location. “They looked independent in two terminals” is not evidence.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: treating worktree creation as cleanup-free</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> every run succeeds, but <code>trees/</code> quietly accumulates full repository copies and stale Git registrations.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> make purge part of the workflow’s lifecycle, not an occasional housekeeping task. Confirm with <code>git worktree list</code>. The source also contains a latent fallback bug: <code>remove_worktree</code> calls <code>shutil.rmtree</code> without importing <code>shutil</code>. That is a reason to inspect cleanup failures—not a reason to abandon isolation. The three-way validator still checks state, filesystem, and Git registration before dependent phases proceed.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>First-class isolation changes the amount of code, not the contract</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p>Modern agent environments increasingly provide worktree-isolated tasks, sandboxed execution, or remote environments directly. The TAC-7 implementation remains pedagogically valuable because you can see path ownership, port allocation, installation, and teardown. If a platform abstracts those pieces, require the same guarantees and evidence.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <div class="video-cards video-cards--single">
+            <a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fop_yxV-mPo">
+              <span class="video-card__type">Operationalizes the end-state</span>
+              <strong>Codebase Singularity</strong>
+              <p>Pairs with the point at which a codebase begins to carry parallel engineering work through its own agentic layer.</p>
+              <span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span>
+            </a>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Isolation retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. An ADW maps to index 7. What port pair is tried first?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Backend 9107 and frontend 9207.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What three surfaces does <code>validate_worktree</code> compare?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The state must contain <code>worktree_path</code>, the directory must exist on the filesystem, and <code>git worktree list</code> must know about it.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is “Git worktrees” an incomplete answer to the isolation lesson?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Dan treats worktrees as one implementation. Docker or E2B can satisfy the durable requirement: isolated, parallel, observable, disposable agent environments.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Draw the boundary around one running ADW.</strong> Explain to an infrastructure engineer what the ID owns, how the first port pair is selected, what happens on collision, how the environment is installed, and how it is removed. Then name one non-worktree implementation with the same contract.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if your boundary prevents branch, process, port, and state collision.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-state">
+        <span class="section-number">04</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The state object is pipeline memory outside the context window</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">A fresh review agent should not need the implementation agent’s conversation. It needs a durable record of which issue, branch, plan, worktree, ports, class, and compute policy belong to the run.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>Phase two cannot inherit phase one’s mind</h3>
+          <p>The planner creates a branch and a plan. The implementor begins in a new process with a single-purpose prompt. Where does it learn the branch name and plan path? If the answer is “repeat the whole planner transcript,” you have broken TAC-6’s focus tactic and coupled every phase to an expanding context. If the answer is “the human tells it,” the human has returned as pipeline memory.</p>
+          <p>A long workflow needs continuity, but continuity does not require one long-lived agent. TAC-7 places continuity in a small JSON state object, allowing each phase agent to remain fresh and narrow. The workflow remembers; the agent focuses.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — ten fields tell the run’s story</p>
+          <h3>A paper trail a fresh agent can load</h3>
+          <div class="state-grid" aria-label="Ten ADW state fields">
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>adw_id</code><p>Identity and directory key for the run.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>issue_number</code><p>The GitHub request and reporting surface.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>branch_name</code><p>The Git branch created for the work.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>plan_file</code><p>The specification produced by planning.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>issue_class</code><p>Chore, bug, feature, or another routed class.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>worktree_path</code><p>The isolated checkout owned by the run.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>backend_port</code><p>The run’s available backend port.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>frontend_port</code><p>The run’s available frontend port.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>model_set</code><p>The base or heavy compute policy.</p></article>
+            <article class="state-grid__item"><code>all_adws</code><p>The workflow phases recorded for KPI attempts.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — load, contribute, save</p>
+          <h3>Every phase changes only what it owns</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t0890-adw-state-json.png" alt="An ADW state JSON file open in the editor with issue, branch, worktree, port, model, and workflow fields" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">14:50</span><code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/adw_state.json</code> is pipeline memory and part of the ship gate. It lives outside every phase agent’s context.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The course’s <code>ADWState</code> begins with <code>adw_id</code>. Its <code>update</code> method accepts only the ten core fields. Saving constructs a validated <code>ADWStateData</code> object and writes formatted JSON beneath <code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/</code>. Loading reads that file, validates it, and recreates the state container. Each phase therefore follows the same modest protocol: load prior state, add its contribution, save.</p>
+          <p>Planning establishes the issue, class, branch, plan, worktree, ports, and model set. Later phases load those facts instead of rediscovering or re-prompting them. <code>all_adws</code> accumulates workflow identifiers without duplicates. The documentation/KPI phase can count that list as attempts. The ship phase can ask whether the run has the minimum complete record required to merge.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>ADW state object</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s term</p>
+            <p><strong>ADW state object:</strong> the durable per-run record that lets single-purpose phase agents coordinate, replay work, and validate readiness without sharing one context window.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>In plain English, it is a baton. The planner hands the implementor a plan path, branch, and environment without handing over its whole conversation. The implementor hands later phases a changed worktree without telling them how it reasoned. The baton is small enough to inspect and strict enough to gate.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — separate total state from ship state</p>
+          <h3>Ten fields exist; eight are merge prerequisites</h3>
+          <p>The state model contains ten fields, but <code>validate_state_completeness</code> in <code>adw_ship_iso.py</code> explicitly requires eight: <code>adw_id</code>, <code>issue_number</code>, <code>branch_name</code>, <code>plan_file</code>, <code>issue_class</code>, <code>worktree_path</code>, <code>backend_port</code>, and <code>frontend_port</code>. <code>model_set</code> and <code>all_adws</code> remain part of the record but are not in that required-field set.</p>
+          <pre><code>expected_fields = {
+    "adw_id", "issue_number", "branch_name", "plan_file",
+    "issue_class", "worktree_path", "backend_port", "frontend_port"
+}</code></pre>
+          <p>If a learner runs planning only and then calls ship, the state file may exist while several required values remain null. The ship workflow reports the missing names and exits before any merge command. This is a diagnostic gift: state completeness tells you which upstream transition never completed.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — trace ownership field by field</p>
+          <h3>Annotate a real state file</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>During the first lab SDLC run, print <code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/adw_state.json</code>.</li>
+            <li>Beside every field, write which phase first populated it.</li>
+            <li>Mark the eight fields ship requires.</li>
+            <li>For <code>all_adws</code>, count the entries and compare the count with the KPI’s attempts value after documentation.</li>
+            <li>Explain which facts belong in persistent state and which details should remain in phase logs.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass if a fresh reviewer could use your annotated state without receiving the planner’s transcript.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: making state a transcript landfill</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> every agent message, intermediate hypothesis, and tool response is copied into a giant state blob so later phases “have context.” The JSON becomes another context window, hard to validate and harder to replay.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> persist coordination facts and externally useful outcomes, not entire reasoning histories. Keep detailed JSONL and logs beneath the run directory. State answers “where and what next?” Logs answer “what happened inside this phase?” This division preserves focus and observability at the same time.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>State remains explicit even when orchestration becomes native</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p>Agent SDKs and workflow platforms can manage sessions, structured outputs, retries, and task state. That is useful plumbing, not permission to make state invisible. Preserve a small, inspectable per-run record with stable identifiers, environment ownership, artifacts, policy choices, and gate status. A platform’s internal session is not a substitute for your domain’s workflow state.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">Durable lesson: keep pipeline memory outside any single model context.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="State object retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name all ten core fields without opening the code.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>adw_id</code>, <code>issue_number</code>, <code>branch_name</code>, <code>plan_file</code>, <code>issue_class</code>, <code>worktree_path</code>, <code>backend_port</code>, <code>frontend_port</code>, <code>model_set</code>, and <code>all_adws</code>.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Which two are not in ship’s eight-field completeness set?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>model_set</code> and <code>all_adws</code>.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What tactic would a single shared mega-context violate?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>TAC-6’s one-agent, one-prompt, one-purpose focus tactic.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Explain the state object as a baton passed through six runners.</strong> Name what planning adds, what later phases read, why <code>all_adws</code> matters to KPIs, and why ship treats missing values as evidence of an incomplete run.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if you preserve both focused agents and a replayable workflow.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-models">
+        <span class="section-number">05</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Model sets turn compute allocation into one dense keyword</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The random-data feature and a text patch should not automatically spend the same compute. TAC-7 encodes a selective base/heavy policy once and lets an issue invoke it with <code>model_set heavy</code>.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>“Use the strongest model everywhere” is not a compute strategy</h3>
+          <p>Classification extracts a few structured fields. Implementation may need to reason across backend, frontend, and tests. Review must compare evidence to a plan. If every phase automatically receives the most expensive model, simple extraction consumes the same class of compute as implementation. If every phase receives the cheapest model, the difficult phase becomes the weak link.</p>
+          <p>The engineering problem is allocation: decide which cognitive jobs benefit from a heavier model, encode that decision centrally, and make the workflow invoke it without restating 18 choices in every issue.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — one map, eighteen phase decisions</p>
+          <h3>Heavy is selective, not universal</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Escalate on heavy · 8</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><code>/implement</code>, <code>/resolve_failed_test</code>, <code>/resolve_failed_e2e_test</code>, <code>/document</code>, <code>/chore</code>, <code>/bug</code>, <code>/feature</code>, <code>/patch</code>.</p></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Stay base · 10</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>Classification, branch naming, tests, review, commit, PR, worktree installation, and KPI tracking remain on the base model in the source map.</p></div></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — issue text becomes policy</p>
+          <h3>From <code>model_set heavy</code> to one phase’s model argument</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t1010-model-map-agents-py.png" alt="Python model-selection map with base and heavy choices for eighteen slash commands" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">16:50</span>The course pauses on the map that turns one issue keyword into eighteen phase decisions. Its shape is durable; the model aliases are period detail.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The issue body carries the keyword. <code>/classify_adw</code> recognizes base or heavy, defaults to base when absent, and returns the choice with the workflow route. Planning persists it in <code>adw_state.json</code>. Later, <code>get_model_for_slash_command</code> loads the state, looks up the current slash command in <code>SLASH_COMMAND_MODEL_MAP</code>, and selects the configured value.</p>
+          <p>In the video, the heavy random-data feature spends 27 minutes on the heavier implementation path and produces roughly 300 lines. Classification and other structural steps do not escalate merely because the feature is heavy. The keyword is information-dense: two words encode a central policy applied consistently across the pipeline.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>Model set and IDK</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Dan’s vocabulary</p>
+            <p><strong>Model set:</strong> a named base/heavy allocation policy. <strong>IDK:</strong> an information-dense keyword such as <code>model_set heavy</code> or “think hard” that a prepared prompt expands into a larger instruction or policy.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>The keyword works only because the receiving prompts and code recognize it. An unexplained word is not dense; it is ambiguous. The density comes from a committed shared meaning. This is why model allocation belongs in a source-controlled map rather than in the operator’s memory.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — trace two commands under one set</p>
+          <h3>Heavy implementation, base classification</h3>
+          <p>Suppose the issue includes <code>adw_sdlc_iso model_set heavy</code>. The classifier runs with the model chosen for <code>/classify_adw</code>: in the course map, both base and heavy point to <code>sonnet</code>. State now records <code>"model_set": "heavy"</code>. When the implementor creates an <code>AgentTemplateRequest</code> for <code>/implement</code>, the same map returns <code>opus</code>. A later <code>/review</code> request loads the same heavy state but still resolves to <code>sonnet</code>.</p>
+          <pre><code>/classify_adw  heavy → sonnet
+/implement     heavy → opus
+/review        heavy → sonnet</code></pre>
+          <p>The set does not name one model for the run. It names a mapping across jobs.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — prove the knob turned</p>
+          <h3>Inspect the run, not the issue label</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>File the lab’s heavy random-row issue.</li>
+            <li>Confirm <code>model_set</code> is <code>heavy</code> in state.</li>
+            <li>Inspect <code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/sdlc_implementor/raw_output.jsonl</code> or the issue’s “Model Set” evidence.</li>
+            <li>Record the implementation model.</li>
+            <li>Compare it with classification, test, and review evidence.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass only when the implementor escalated and at least one structural phase stayed base. A keyword in an issue is an instruction; the log is proof.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: making “heavy” a superstition</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> every issue says <code>model_set heavy</code> because failure feels more expensive than tokens, yet no one knows which phases actually benefit.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> treat the map as an engineering hypothesis. Measure which jobs fail, how resolution behaves, and where extra compute changes outcomes. Keep extractive and deterministic transitions on the base path unless evidence says otherwise. The course’s 8-of-18 split demonstrates selectivity; it does not claim those exact values are eternal.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>Model identifiers expire; policy shape persists</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p>The source aliases <code>sonnet</code> and <code>opus</code> describe the course era. Model families and economics change. Preserve the named allocation knob and update the values through evaluation. A current system may allocate by model, reasoning budget, tool policy, latency tier, or several dimensions; the issue should still invoke a reviewed policy rather than a pile of ad hoc flags.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">Period detail: model names. Durable idea: per-command compute policy stored in workflow state.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Model set retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. How many commands are in the map, and how many change on heavy?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Eighteen commands total; eight change from the base to the heavier model.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Which phase reads the issue’s keyword, and where is the result stored?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>/classify_adw</code> extracts it; <code>model_set</code> is persisted in <code>adw_state.json</code>.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Does heavy mean every phase runs on the heavier model?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. It selects the heavy column of a per-command map. Ten of the eighteen commands remain on the base model in this source.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Teach <code>model_set heavy</code> to a finance-minded engineering lead.</strong> Explain why it is not “maximum model everywhere,” trace it from issue to state to command lookup, and describe how you would revisit the map when model families change.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if your explanation connects cost, phase difficulty, proof, and central policy.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-review">
+        <span class="section-number">06</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Review is proof of value, not a second test run</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Tests ask whether the system’s executable contracts pass. Review asks whether the thing built is the thing requested—and posts visible proof into the place where the work is judged.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>“The tests passed” does not answer the product question</h3>
+          <p>A drop-zone feature can pass every backend test while rendering the overlay in the wrong region. A button rename can preserve behavior while changing the wrong label. An agent’s “done” summary can be internally consistent and externally false. Tests prove contracts that exist; they do not automatically prove the requested experience.</p>
+          <p>The review system closes that gap by booting the built application, exercising critical paths, comparing observed behavior with the plan, and returning screenshots plus structured findings. Review earns trust because it produces inspectable evidence, not because a second agent says “looks good.”</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — evidence lands in the issue</p>
+          <h3>Four screenshots where the reviewer already works</h3>
+          <ul class="proof-strip" aria-label="Review evidence sequence">
+            <li>boot isolated app</li><li>drive critical path</li><li>capture 1–5 images</li><li>classify findings</li><li>upload or keep local paths</li><li>post to issue</li>
+          </ul>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — inspect the request in the running app</p>
+          <h3>The review agent enters the worktree as a user</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t1290-review-screenshot-proof.png" alt="GitHub issue review comment showing four captured screenshots and visible feature proof" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">21:30</span>The issue reports four captured screenshots. Proof lands next to the request, so the review surface contains both expected and observed behavior.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p><code>adw_review_iso.py</code> loads state, validates the worktree, starts the application on that run’s ports, and invokes a focused review prompt with Playwright MCP. The reviewer selects critical paths, captures between one and five numbered screenshots, and returns structured results. The images are uploaded to Cloudflare R2 when its four environment variables exist; otherwise local file paths are the expected fallback.</p>
+          <p>Findings use three severities: <code>skippable</code>, <code>tech_debt</code>, and <code>blocker</code>. Only a blocker makes review unsuccessful. A failing review is not merely announced. The workflow can patch and re-review, as the video’s “attempt 1/3” evidence shows. This is TAC-5’s closed loop operating inside TAC-7’s autonomous path.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>Proof of value</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">The review question</p>
+            <p><strong>Proof of value:</strong> evidence that answers “Is what we built what we asked for?” rather than repeating “Does the code execute?”</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>Proof must be easy to consume. If screenshots remain buried inside a worktree no human or downstream gate visits, they do not reduce review friction. Posting them to the issue joins the request, run trace, result, and evidence in one surface. The lesson’s next suggested evolution is video uploaded to a bucket and posted as a URL.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — grade a finding set</p>
+          <h3>Two debts and one cosmetic note do not block</h3>
+          <p>Suppose the reviewer returns one <code>skippable</code> note about a noncritical animation and two <code>tech_debt</code> items about internal organization, but screenshots prove the requested drop-zone behavior. Under the course contract, review succeeds because there is no <code>blocker</code>. The debts remain useful information; they do not masquerade as release-stopping defects.</p>
+          <p>Now suppose the overlay covers only half the required region. That contradicts the requested behavior and becomes a blocker. Review returns failure, the resolution loop patches, and review runs again. Severity is therefore part of loop control. If every comment blocks, the pipeline cries wolf. If nothing blocks, evidence cannot protect the ship.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — compare two evidence questions</p>
+          <h3>Write a test claim and a review claim</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Choose the lab’s footer feature.</li>
+            <li>Write one executable claim a unit or integration test should prove.</li>
+            <li>Write one visible product claim a screenshot should prove.</li>
+            <li>After the run, point to the exact test output and exact screenshot supporting each claim.</li>
+            <li>Classify one hypothetical imperfection at each severity.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass if the screenshot claim cannot be replaced by “tests passed,” and the test claim cannot be replaced by a pretty image.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: automating evidence nobody reads</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> the workflow dutifully captures screenshots, but the team approves from the green check and never opens them. Presence appears to fall while actual review has disappeared rather than been embedded.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> make evidence part of the decision contract. Define critical paths, keep the set small, require captions or numbered claims, and sample the proof during the trust-building period. ZTE becomes legitimate when the proof system repeatedly agrees with human inspection—not when evidence generation exists as ceremony.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>From screenshots toward richer review products</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p>The repo already configures Playwright video at 1920×1080 under <code>videos/</code>. Dan names video as the next obvious proof surface. Richer review can include trace artifacts, accessibility evidence, or several independent reviews, but the invariant is the same: proof must map the request to observed behavior and land where the release decision happens.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">Do not add more evidence by default; add the smallest artifact that makes the claim inspectable.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Review retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What question separates review from testing?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Testing asks “Does it work against executable contracts?” Review asks “Is what we built what we asked for?”</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Name the three severity levels. Which blocks?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p><code>skippable</code>, <code>tech_debt</code>, and <code>blocker</code>. Only <code>blocker</code> makes review unsuccessful.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Are local screenshot paths evidence of a broken R2 uploader?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. Without the four Cloudflare R2 variables, upload disables silently and local paths are the expected fallback.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Convince a test-focused engineer that review is not redundant.</strong> Use the drop-zone example, explain severity as control flow, show why proof belongs on the issue, and name the failure mode where screenshot automation becomes empty ceremony.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if you defend both executable checks and product evidence without collapsing one into the other.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-bottleneck">
+        <span class="section-number">07</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">When review stops finding defects, the human becomes the bottleneck</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The tenth flawless review of the same chore class may not be safety. It may be queueing delay. TAC-7 does not ask you to guess which is true; it gives you attempts, size, presence, and streak as evidence.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>The approval that adds no information</h3>
+          <p>A workflow changes the same class of configuration value every week. The plan is bounded. Tests pass. Review evidence matches the request. The resolution loop has not found a blocker in ten runs. Yet every run waits hours for a human to open the screenshot, nod, and press merge. The approval still feels responsible because it is human. Operationally, it contributes no new information and dominates lead time.</p>
+          <p>Dan names this moment carefully: the human-in-the-loop review model is a bottleneck <em>for this class of problems</em>. That qualification keeps the realization from becoming a manifesto against review. The question is whether your touch changes outcomes often enough to justify its cost.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — read the committed receipt</p>
+          <h3>One real run preserved in <code>agentic_kpis.md</code></h3>
+          <div class="kpi-board" role="img" aria-label="Committed KPI values for ADW cc73faf1">
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>1</strong><span>attempt</span></article>
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>39</strong><span>plan lines</span></article>
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>1.0</strong><span>average presence</span></article>
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>1</strong><span>current streak</span></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>The snapshot’s row is ADW <code>cc73faf1</code>, issue #47, class <code>/chore</code>, diff <code>274/5/10</code> (added/removed/files). Its committed specification carries the trigger text that changes “Upload Data” to “Upload” through <code>adw_sdlc_ZTE_iso</code>. It is repository evidence of presence one, not the video’s issue #62.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — convert intuition into a decision</p>
+          <h3>The KPI shape of ZTE</h3>
+          <p>At 30:45, the lesson turns from machinery to realization. Dan says he is not catching bugs and not adding value outside the layer for a class of work. At 31:56 he describes the desired KPI shape: attempts at one, size scaling upward, streak increasing, and presence dropping to one. These are not vanity metrics. Together they ask whether the workflow succeeds on the first try, handles meaningful scope, stays reliable over time, and requires fewer touches.</p>
+          <p><code>app_docs/agentic_kpis.md</code> is updated in the documentation phase. <code>all_adws</code> contributes to attempts, and the KPI prompt forces arithmetic through <code>python -c</code> rather than relying on unaudited mental math. The record is modest, but it changes the conversation from “I trust agents” to “Here is the class, history, size, and presence.”</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — Dan term + plain English</p>
+          <h3>Presence and streak</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Operational metrics</p>
+            <p><strong>Presence</strong> counts human touchpoints per shipped unit. <strong>Streak</strong> records consecutive successful runs. Presence says how much of you the workflow consumes; streak says whether reliability persists.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>The metrics need each other. Presence one with a streak of one is a successful experiment, not proof of universal readiness. A long streak at presence two may show that review is ready to become a sampled audit. A large diff with low presence may demonstrate scale, or it may indicate review risk; size is context, not a score to maximize.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — decide whether to drop one review</p>
+          <h3>Compare two histories, not two feelings</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Class A · styling chores</h3><p>Eight clean reviewed runs</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>Attempts usually one. Screenshots consistently match. No blocker resolution. Human reviews report no added findings. Candidate: one bounded uppercase-ZTE experiment with deterministic gates.</p></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Class B · schema features</h3><p>Four mixed runs</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><p>Two required test resolution. One review found a blocker. Plans vary widely. Candidate: remain out-loop and improve tests/review before reducing presence.</p></div></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>The numbers in this comparison illustrate a reasoning pattern, not claims about the course snapshot. The repository provides only one KPI row. A learner must build their own history during and after the lab.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — audit your review’s marginal value</p>
+          <h3>Write a finding, not a feeling</h3>
+          <p>For each capstone run, record the problem class, attempts, plan size, diff size, presence, whether review changed the outcome, and whether a blocker appeared. After four runs, write exactly one sentence: “For class ___, my review did/did not add value because ___.” Do not authorize a production policy from four course runs; use the sentence to identify the next experiment.</p>
+          <p>Pass when your conclusion could be disproved by the next run. “Agents are good now” and “humans must always review” are beliefs, not testable class-level claims.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: cherry-picking the successful streak</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> failed attempts disappear from the ledger, “observation” quietly includes manual corrections, or a different problem class is counted as supporting evidence.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> define the class and presence-counting rule before the run. Preserve failed attempts. Keep video runtime values separate from committed snapshot values. A KPI earns permission only when its inputs are honest and comparable.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>Move from all-review to sampled or risk-tiered review</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice</p>
+            <p>A mature application can use deterministic CI, branch rules, risk labels, audit sampling, and rollback signals around agentic review. TAC-7’s contribution is the decision axis: reduce human presence only where evidence shows the touch has become redundant. More controls should make the decision explicit, not hide it behind a green dashboard.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">The course names the future direction: deterministic deploy checks and parallel review agents whose outputs can be fused.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Bottleneck and KPI retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Which KPI directly records how touched a run was?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Presence. ZTE targets a value of one.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What are the committed snapshot values for ADW <code>cc73faf1</code>?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Issue #47, <code>/chore</code>, attempts 1, plan 39 lines, diff 274/5/10, average presence 1.0, current streak 1.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why can’t one clean ZTE row prove all features are ready?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The evidence belongs to one run and one problem class. Readiness is per class and depends on a history, not a global belief.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Tell a cautious manager when a human review becomes latency rather than safety.</strong> Use attempts, size, presence, streak, blocker history, and the phrase “for this class of problems.” Then name one reason to restore review.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if the decision is evidence-gated and reversible.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-tactic">
+        <span class="section-number">08</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #7: Target Zero-Touch Engineering</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">TAC-1 told you to stop hand-typing. TAC-7 removes the next transition you learned to perform: review. The word “Target” keeps the move disciplined, incremental, and class-specific.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>You replaced coding with a review queue</h3>
+          <p>After six tactics, agents can plan, build, test, repair, review, document, and open a PR while you are away. But every successful run still waits for you. You stopped being the typist and became the approval server. If the system’s evidence is reliable and your repeated approvals add no findings, the final review transition is the next leverage point.</p>
+          <p>The provocation mirrors TAC-1. “Stop coding” never meant “stop producing or understanding code.” “Stop reviewing” never means “remove evaluation.” It means move evaluation into tests, review agents, proof artifacts, resolution loops, state gates, and shipping controls so the human does not repeat a zero-information approval.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the tactic card</p>
+          <h3>Presence two becomes presence one</h3>
+          <div class="kpi-board" role="img" aria-label="The ZTE tactic reduces human presence from two touchpoints to one">
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>2</strong><span>out-loop: prompt + review</span></article>
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>→</strong><span>target one proven class</span></article>
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>1</strong><span>ZTE: prompt only</span></article>
+            <article class="kpi-board__item"><strong>PITE</strong><span>evaluation stays embedded</span></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — PITER drops a letter</p>
+          <h3>The review step leaves the human framework, not the pipeline</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t1912-tactic-target-zte.png" alt="Course tactic card titled Target Zero-Touch Engineering with daily actions and presence moving from two to one" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">31:52</span>Tactic 07 arrives only after the bottleneck argument. Its daily actions connect parallel pipelines, isolated environments, PITER → PITE, and review that no longer requires a human touch.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>Dan’s reveal moves quickly. At 31:19, out-loop PITER becomes PITE: Prompt input, Trigger, Environment. The human Review drops off the end. At 31:33 he calls the posture “YOLO mode” and “maximum confidence mode.” At 31:50 he names “Target Zero-Touch Engineering” as the ultimate agentic coding North Star. The playful language sits on top of seven lessons of controls.</p>
+          <p>Dan immediately voices the objection at 32:37: the app is a toy and auto-merging is too far. He agrees about the app, then redirects toward experimentation. Start with chores, move to bugs, then features. You learn where ZTE becomes real by trying on bounded classes, not by asserting a future policy from a slide.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — tactic and code use two expansions</p>
+          <h3>Zero-Touch Engineering / Zero Touch Execution</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Naming duality</p>
+            <p><strong>Zero-Touch Engineering</strong> is the course tactic. <strong>Zero Touch Execution</strong> appears in code, docstrings, and bot comments. Both are ZTE; preserve the distinction rather than “correcting” the source.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p><strong>Target</strong> is load-bearing. ZTE is a North Star pursued one class and one day at a time. Without that word, the tactic sounds like “auto-merge everything now,” which the lesson neither demonstrates nor recommends.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — transform PITER into PITE</p>
+          <h3>What disappears, and what remains?</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>PITER · out-loop</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>P</strong><code>prompt input</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>I</strong><code>trigger</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>T/E</strong><code>isolated environment</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>R</strong><code>human review + merge</code></div></div></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>PITE · ZTE target</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>P</strong><code>prompt input</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>I</strong><code>trigger</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>T/E</strong><code>isolated environment</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Embedded</strong><code>tests + review + gates + ship</code></div></div></div></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>The “R” removed from the human framework is not replaced by nothing. The software pipeline still reviews, resolves blockers, produces evidence, validates state and environment, and refuses the merge on defined failures.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — choose one target class</p>
+          <h3>Write a ZTE readiness contract</h3>
+          <p>For the lab’s background-color chore, write four lines before launch: class boundary, required tests, required visual proof, and abort conditions. Add the presence you expect. After the run, compare the evidence with the contract. Do not broaden the result to other chores, bugs, or features.</p>
+          <p>Pass when another learner can tell which changes qualify and which superficially similar changes must remain reviewed.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Two ways to miss the word “Target”</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Global YOLO</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Failure:</strong> enable auto-merge for every issue because one chore shipped.</p><p><strong>Repair:</strong> keep class boundaries and require separate evidence histories.</p></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Permanent standstill</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Failure:</strong> reject every experiment because the demo is a toy.</p><p><strong>Repair:</strong> choose low-stakes, bounded work and instrument the attempt.</p></div></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>Keep the target; strengthen the deterministic perimeter</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice</p>
+            <p>A production target can add branch protection, required CI, scoped credentials, merge queues, staged deployment, canaries, and rollback automation around the course pipeline. These controls do not change the tactic. They make “prompt only” mean the human designed the policy in advance rather than approving each unit afterward.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">Treat the uppercase interlock as a course-era teaching artifact, not a complete production guardrail.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Target ZTE retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What does PITER lose, and what does the workflow retain?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It loses the human R/review touchpoint. The pipeline retains tests, agentic review, blocker resolution, evidence, state validation, and ship gates.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Why is “Target” load-bearing?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It makes ZTE a per-class North Star earned through evidence, not a global day-one mode.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What two expansions of ZTE appear in the lesson artifacts?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Zero-Touch Engineering for the tactic and Zero Touch Execution in code and bot output.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Respond to “Auto-merging agents is insane.”</strong> Agree with the risk, then explain “Target,” chores → bugs → features, PITER → PITE, embedded evaluation, and the one-class experiment you would permit first.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if your answer is neither reckless nor immobilized.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-gates">
+        <span class="section-number">09</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Read the ZTE source and count the ways it refuses to merge</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead"><code>adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py</code> is not an incantation. It is a six-phase orchestrator with explicit abort behavior, followed by a ship workflow that validates state and environment before touching Git.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>Fear stays vague until you enumerate control flow</h3>
+          <p>“The agent can merge to main” sounds like unlimited authority. That sentence hides every precondition and failure path. The responsible response is not reassurance; it is source inspection. Which phases can stop the run? Which failure continues? What must exist before Git runs? Which merge strategy is actually used? Does browser E2E run? What restores repository state on failure?</p>
+          <p>TAC-7 rewards countable answers. The source is short enough to audit, and the audit surfaces two facts the README alone would mislead you about.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the pipeline announces its authority</p>
+          <h3>Six phases and an explicit warning</h3>
+          <div class="guardrail-stack" aria-label="ZTE abort and merge gates">
+            <article class="guardrail-stack__gate"><strong>Explicit uppercase route</strong><p><code>/classify_adw</code> downgrades lowercase <code>zte</code> to reviewed SDLC.</p></article>
+            <article class="guardrail-stack__gate"><strong>Test return code</strong><p>Failure posts “ZTE Aborted” and exits.</p></article>
+            <article class="guardrail-stack__gate"><strong>Review return code</strong><p>Failure posts “ZTE Aborted” and exits.</p></article>
+            <article class="guardrail-stack__gate"><strong>Eight complete state fields</strong><p>Missing values are named; ship exits before merge.</p></article>
+            <article class="guardrail-stack__gate"><strong>Three-way worktree validation</strong><p>State, filesystem, and Git registration must agree.</p></article>
+            <article class="guardrail-stack__gate"><strong>Git operations must all succeed</strong><p>Fetch, checkout, pull, <code>--no-ff</code> merge, and push are checked.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — follow the happy and unhappy paths</p>
+          <h3>Plan → build → test → review → document → ship</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t2135-zte-initiation-issue.png" alt="GitHub issue initiation comment listing six ZTE phases and warning that code will automatically merge" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">35:35</span>The initiation comment makes authority visible before work begins: six phases and a warning that passing gates lead to automatic merge.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>The ZTE orchestrator ensures an ADW ID, posts the six-phase warning, and launches each isolated script in order. Plan or build failure exits. Test always receives <code>--skip-e2e</code>; a nonzero result posts a cancellation comment and exits. Review failure does the same. Documentation is different: failure prints a warning and continues because the source explicitly says documentation should not block shipping. Ship failure posts manual-merge advice and exits.</p>
+          <p>Inside <code>adw_ship_iso.py</code>, the workflow requires both issue number and ADW ID, loads state, appends its workflow name, checks environment variables, and posts status. It validates the eight fields, then the worktree. Only then does <code>manual_merge_to_main</code> run in the repository root: save the current branch, fetch origin, checkout main, pull main, merge the feature branch with <code>--no-ff</code> and a “via ADW Ship workflow” message, push, and restore the original branch. Failure paths attempt restoration.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — source vocabulary</p>
+          <h3>Zero Touch Execution and the final approval step</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Source contract</p>
+            <p><strong>Ship phase:</strong> the course code’s “final approval step,” implemented as state/worktree validation followed by manual Git merge and push from the main repository.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>“Manual” in <code>manual_merge_to_main</code> means direct Git commands rather than a GitHub PR merge API. It does not mean a person types them during ZTE. The distinction matters when the README says “approves the PR + squash”: the shipping source neither approves through the API nor squashes.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — reconcile three surprising facts</p>
+          <h3>Docs drift, E2E omission, and the uppercase interlock</h3>
+          <table>
+            <thead><tr><th>Claim you might repeat</th><th>What source proves</th><th>Operational consequence</th></tr></thead>
+            <tbody>
+              <tr><td>“Ship squash-merges the PR.”</td><td><code>git merge &lt;branch&gt; --no-ff -m ...</code>, then push.</td><td><code>git log</code> should show a merge commit.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>“Full SDLC includes browser E2E.”</td><td>Both SDLC orchestrators hardcode <code>--skip-e2e</code>.</td><td>Run <code>adw_test_iso.py</code> standalone without the flag for E2E.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>“Lowercase zte is another spelling.”</td><td><code>/classify_adw</code> requires explicit uppercase <code>ZTE</code>.</td><td>Lowercase routes to reviewed <code>adw_sdlc_iso</code>.</td></tr>
+            </tbody>
+          </table>
+          <p>These are not trivia questions. They change what evidence you think exists and what authority the run exercised.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — perform a source-first gate audit</p>
+          <h3>Use four files, produce one control-flow list</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>Read <code>.claude/commands/classify_adw.md</code> and locate the uppercase warning.</li>
+            <li>Read <code>adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py</code> and mark every nonzero-return exit plus the documentation exception.</li>
+            <li>Read <code>adw_ship_iso.py</code> and copy the eight required fields and Git command order.</li>
+            <li>Read <code>adw_modules/worktree_ops.py</code> and name the three validation surfaces.</li>
+            <li>Write where standalone E2E must be invoked.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass if your list can predict exactly what the lowercase interlock and a missing <code>plan_file</code> will do before you run them.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: equating several prompt-level checks with production safety</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you count six gates and conclude the pipeline is universally safe, even though browser E2E is skipped and the course app equates “production” with main.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> say exactly what the gates prove. They defend routing, unit-test success, agentic review success, state completeness, environment consistency, and Git transition success in this repository. A real deployment may require required external CI, protected branches, scoped credentials, deployment verification, canaries, and rollback. Count controls without inflating their scope.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>The subprocess wrapper is the SDK, hand-rolled</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p><code>adws/adw_modules/agent.py</code> launches <code>claude -p</code>, parses stream JSON, captures sessions, and retries on a 1/3/5-second ladder. Current Agent SDKs provide structured output, hooks, sessions, and error handling directly. Replacing the wrapper can shrink code while leaving the ADW phase boundaries, state, gates, and evidence contracts intact.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <div class="video-cards video-cards--single">
+            <a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6fCqj4xFCZI">
+              <span class="video-card__type">Bridges course-era embedding to current practice</span>
+              <strong>How Claude Code CHANGED Engineering Forever</strong>
+              <p>Use after the source audit to separate the durable workflow architecture from the period-specific subprocess wrapper.</p>
+              <span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span>
+            </a>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="ZTE gate retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Which failing phase does not stop shipping?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Documentation. The orchestrator warns and continues.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What happens before any merge command?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The ship workflow validates eight required state fields and validates the worktree against state, filesystem, and Git registration.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What merge strategy does the code use?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>A direct <code>git merge --no-ff</code> with a “via ADW Ship workflow” merge message, followed by push to origin/main.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. Does ZTE’s test phase run browser E2E?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>No. The orchestrator always passes <code>--skip-e2e</code>. E2E must be run through standalone <code>adw_test_iso.py</code> without that flag.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Walk a security reviewer through the exact ZTE control flow.</strong> Include the uppercase route, phase aborts, documentation exception, eight fields, three-way worktree validation, Git command order, branch restoration, and the skipped-E2E limitation.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if every reassurance is tied to a source-backed control and every limitation remains visible.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-ship">
+        <span class="section-number">10</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">One prompt reaches main—with no second human touch</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The ZTE demo’s power is not the color change. It is the continuity: one issue prompt triggers six isolated phases, evidence appears, blockers can abort, and a passing branch merges into main without Dan returning to approve it.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>Small work is where expensive transitions look most absurd</h3>
+          <p>A designer asks for a background-color change. The application edit is trivial, but the organizational path is not: ticket, branch, change, test, visual check, documentation, PR, merge. When the transition ceremony dwarfs the implementation, “I can do it faster myself” keeps the organization permanently in-loop. The low-stakes class is exactly where a complete zero-touch experiment can reveal whether the layer is real.</p>
+          <p>Dan calls styling whims low-hanging fruit and says this is not where the engineer should spend time. The smallness is not an evasion of production complexity. It is experimental control: choose a bounded class so the system’s transitions, evidence, and authority—not the code difficulty—are the variable.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — the changed application on main</p>
+          <h3>The visual result after <code>git pull origin main</code></h3>
+          <div class="workflow workflow--spaced" aria-label="One prompt reaches the main branch through six gated phases">
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Prompt</strong><code>issue #62</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Build</strong><code>isolated tree</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Prove</strong><code>test + review</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Ship</strong><code>validate + merge</code></div>
+            <div class="workflow__step"><strong>Main</strong><code>pull + observe</code></div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — issue #62 traverses the whole layer</p>
+          <h3>Prompt, warning, proof, merge, pull</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t2958-zte-shipped-app.jpg" alt="Natural Language SQL application showing the background color shipped by the ZTE workflow" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">49:18</span>After the issue closes and main is pulled, the visible background change lands the story: one prompt traversed the full layer without a second approval touch.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <p>At 34:38 Dan files “Update the background color” with <code>/chore adw_sdlc_ZTE_iso</code>. The uppercase token is intentional. At 35:31 the issue receives the six-phase initiation comment and automatic-merge warning. Plan, build, unit test, review, and documentation execute on the base model set. At 44:16 review screenshots report that the background is green. Ship validates the run and merges.</p>
+          <p>At 48:31 Dan reports that the issue is closed and the change is deployed. At 49:14 he pulls origin/main and shows the application. His summary is the presence calculation: all he did was write the prompt. The committed repository preserves a different real ZTE chore—background to light-sky-blue <code>#E0F6FF</code> and “Upload Data” shortened to “Upload”—so the snapshot proves the same class without pretending to contain issue #62.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — production in this lesson</p>
+          <h3>One-prompt-to-production-shaped flow</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Scope the word</p>
+            <p><strong>Production, in tac-7:</strong> the repository’s main branch after ship merges and pushes. Dan explicitly says your equivalent should connect to however staging and production are configured.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>Do not claim that the course performs a universal deployment. It demonstrates the final repository transition and the place where an organization would attach deploy checks. Precision strengthens the lesson: ZTE is about removing a human transition from a system whose actual destination and controls you define.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — calculate presence for the demo</p>
+          <h3>What counts as a touch?</h3>
+          <p>Dan writes issue #62: touch one. The layer classifies, creates the environment, plans, builds, tests, reviews, documents, ships, comments, and closes without asking him for a decision. He later observes and teaches the result, but no production approval or repair is introduced. For the operational run, presence is one.</p>
+          <p>Contrast the earlier drag-and-drop feature. Dan writes the issue, then later runs <code>/in_loop_review</code> and personally exercises it. That run is not ZTE, and his return is not hidden. The two stories establish the counting rule better than a slogan.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — run the uppercase/lowercase pair</p>
+          <h3>Prove both authority and restraint</h3>
+          <ol>
+            <li>In a course clone whose GitHub repository you control, file the uppercase background-color chore from the lab.</li>
+            <li>Capture the initiation warning and phase outcomes.</li>
+            <li>After completion, pull main and inspect <code>git log --oneline -3</code> for the ship merge message.</li>
+            <li>Serve main and verify the requested color.</li>
+            <li>File the lowercase <code>adw_sdlc_zte_iso</code> variant and capture its downgrade to reviewed SDLC.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <p>Pass only with proof of both behaviors. An auto-merge without the interlock experiment demonstrates authority but not your understanding of the safety boundary.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: dismissing the demo because the change is cosmetic</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you argue that a background color says nothing about difficult engineering, so you learn nothing from the run.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> grade the actual hypothesis. The demo tests whether an issue can traverse routing, isolation, planning, implementation, tests, review evidence, documentation, state validation, and merge with presence one. It does not prove that schema migrations or novel features belong at ZTE. Small scope isolates the workflow experiment.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>Connect ship to the deployment system you really have</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice</p>
+            <p>A production-shaped implementation can let the agent propose and validate while protected automation owns merge and deployment. Required checks, a merge queue, environment-scoped credentials, post-deploy probes, canaries, and automatic rollback can all sit after the course’s review gate. Presence can remain one because policy decisions were encoded before the run.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">Do not rename “merge to main” as “deployed” unless your repository’s real delivery system makes that true.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="ZTE ship demo retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What exactly was Dan’s single touch for issue #62?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Writing the issue prompt with the uppercase ZTE route.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What does “production” concretely mean in the course repo?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Main branch after the ship workflow merges and pushes. Dan says an organization should connect that step to its real staging/production delivery path.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is the background-color size a feature of the experiment?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It creates a low-stakes bounded chore class on which to test the complete transition system and presence-one behavior before attempting riskier classes.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Narrate issue #62 as a state machine, not a success story.</strong> Name the input, route, six phases, abort points, evidence, Git transition, visible result, and why the operational presence is one.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if the listener can distinguish observation after the fact from an approval touch during the run.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-secret">
+        <span class="section-number">11</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">The secret: the SDLC was only one composition</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">Seven lessons appeared to teach plan → build → test → review → document → deploy. The payoff is realizing that these are arrangements of reusable primitives, not a sacred pipeline.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>The pipeline becomes dogma</h3>
+          <p>You build one reliable SDLC workflow and send every request through it. A one-word label change receives planning, full tests, browser review, documentation, and deployment ceremony. A review-only audit receives an implementation phase it does not need. A documentation task is forced to pretend it is a feature. Reliability has hardened into rigidity.</p>
+          <p>Dan’s secret is not that the SDLC fails. It is that the SDLC is one useful sentence written with a smaller vocabulary. Mastery means owning the vocabulary well enough to write a different sentence for each engineering problem class.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — atoms, steps, compositions</p>
+          <h3>Any workflow sits to the right of reusable primitives</h3>
+          <figure class="frame-card">
+            <img src="../media/frames/tac-7-zte-secret-of-agentic-engineering/t2765-secret-composable-primitives.png" alt="The Secret slide showing agentic primitives, ADW steps, and composed workflows including any workflow" width="1920" height="1080" loading="lazy">
+            <figcaption><span class="frame-card__t">46:05</span>The course’s payoff slide: primitives feed ADW steps; steps form composed workflows; the SDLC is only one possible composition.</figcaption>
+          </figure>
+          <div class="primitive-map" aria-label="Composable agentic primitives map">
+            <article class="primitive-map__column"><strong>Agentic primitives</strong><ul><li>ADWs</li><li>templates</li><li>plans</li><li>architecture</li><li>tests</li><li>documentation</li><li>types</li><li>stdout</li><li>tools</li><li>prompt</li><li>model</li><li>context</li></ul></article>
+            <article class="primitive-map__column"><strong>ADW steps</strong><ul><li>plan</li><li>build</li><li>test</li><li>review</li><li>document</li><li>deploy</li></ul></article>
+            <article class="primitive-map__column"><strong>Compositions</strong><ul><li>plan → build → test</li><li>build → review → deploy</li><li>test → document</li><li>any workflow</li></ul></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — inspect the 14 scripts</p>
+          <h3>The repository has been teaching composition all along</h3>
+          <p>The <code>adws/</code> directory contains 14 workflow scripts: individual phase workflows plus compositions such as plan/build, plan/build/test, plan/build/review, plan/build/document, plan/build/test/review, full SDLC, SDLC+ZTE ship, patch, and ship. They reuse the same modules for state, GitHub, agents, Git, worktrees, and reporting.</p>
+          <p>The CSV-text bug in the opening demo takes <code>adw_patch_iso</code>. The features take <code>adw_sdlc_iso</code>. Both use isolated environments and the same underlying agentic layer. At 45:58 Dan states the secret twice: tactical agentic coding is not about the software developer lifecycle; it is about composable agentic primitives that can solve any engineering problem class. The SDLC remains relevant, but its arrangement is arbitrary rather than universal.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — the course thesis</p>
+          <h3>Composable agentic primitives</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">The secret in one sentence</p>
+            <p><strong>Own reusable agentic primitives and compose them into the workflow that ships for your codebase and problem class; treat the SDLC as one arrangement, not the objective.</strong></p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>A primitive is useful below the level of a complete workflow: a prompt template, plan artifact, tool boundary, model choice, context packet, test contract, stdout protocol, or state transition. An ADW step packages primitives for one purpose. A composed workflow orders steps and decides which outcomes gate the next transition.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — derive patch and ZTE from the same vocabulary</p>
+          <h3>Two compositions, one layer</h3>
+          <div class="comparison">
+            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>Patch composition</h3><p>One narrow change</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Environment</strong><code>worktree + ports</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Patch plan</strong><code>specs/patch</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Implement</strong><code>focused agent</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>PR</strong><code>human-visible result</code></div></div></div></article>
+            <article class="comparison__card comparison__card--agentic"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>ZTE composition</h3><p>Full evidence + ship</p></div><div class="comparison__body"><div class="workflow workflow--stacked"><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Plan/build</strong><code>stateful phases</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Test/review</strong><code>abort + proof</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Document</strong><code>nonblocking</code></div><div class="workflow__step"><strong>Ship</strong><code>validate + merge</code></div></div></div></article>
+          </div>
+          <p>Calling patch “a smaller SDLC” hides the design insight. It is a different selection and order of primitives for a different problem class. The exact composition that ships in your codebase is the one that matters.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — compose before you code</p>
+          <h3>Design three workflows from the course primitives</h3>
+          <p>On paper, compose a workflow for each: a documentation-only update, a flaky-test diagnosis, and a low-risk UI copy patch. For every composition, name the input, isolated environment requirement, state fields consumed, agent purpose, verification, evidence surface, and terminal action. You may use only primitives and files already present in the tac-7 course codebase; this is design, not an invitation to add new machinery.</p>
+          <p>Pass if the three graphs differ and every omitted phase has a reason.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: hearing “SDLC is a remnant” as “delete the SDLC”</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> you replace one dogma with another, dismantling a working pipeline because the lesson says its arrangement is arbitrary.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> retain compositions that fit their classes. Demote them from universal truth to named tools. A fixed workflow is valuable when it reliably solves a recurring shape. The failure is not having an SDLC; it is lacking the primitives and judgment to choose anything else.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>Committed command prompts evolve into Skills</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Current practice · mid-2026</p>
+            <p>The course packages reusable engineering in 33 command Markdown files, with <code>conditional_docs.md</code> routing relevant context. Agent Skills now offer a first-class package with metadata, on-demand loading, and progressive disclosure. The durable move is to commit reusable expertise and load only what the focused agent needs.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <div class="video-cards video-cards--single">
+            <a class="video-card" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X2ciJedw2vU">
+              <span class="video-card__type">Updates command-file packaging</span>
+              <strong>RAW Agentic Coding: ZERO to Agent SKILL</strong>
+              <p>Connects TAC-7’s committed one-purpose prompts and conditional docs to their current skill-shaped packaging.</p>
+              <span class="video-card__watch">Open video ↗</span>
+            </a>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Composable primitives retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. State the secret in one sentence.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Tactical agentic coding is about composable agentic primitives for any engineering problem class; the SDLC is one possible arrangement.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Name four primitives from the course slide.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Any four of ADWs, templates, plans, architecture, tests, documentation, types, stdout, tools, prompt, model, and context.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is patch not merely “small SDLC”?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It selects and orders a different subset of primitives for a narrow problem class, omitting phases whose ceremony would not add value.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Resolve the apparent rug-pull.</strong> Explain why seven SDLC-centered lessons were still useful if the SDLC was never the point. Use primitives → steps → compositions and compare patch with ZTE.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if the SDLC remains useful but loses its monopoly.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-belief">
+        <span class="section-number">12</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Choose a posture: run, stand still, move, or sprint</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">The technical pieces are copyable. Dan closes by arguing that the limiting factor is whether you believe the agentic layer deserves sustained investment—and whether that belief produces one concrete experiment.</p>
+
+        <div class="move move--hook">
+          <p class="move-label">1 · Hook — concrete failure</p>
+          <h3>Motivation that changes no workflow</h3>
+          <p>You finish the lesson energized, repeat that agentic engineering is the future, and return to a queue where every AI-assisted task still requires continuous prompting and manual transition. Nothing in the system records state, captures proof, or reduces presence. The belief section becomes entertainment rather than a design decision.</p>
+          <p>Dan’s challenge is sharper: if you do not believe agents can run your codebase, you are “cooked.” He is not asking for blind faith in a model. He is asking whether you believe the layer is worth building so models and tools have a reliable environment in which to act.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--picture">
+          <p class="move-label">2 · Picture — four postures toward the future</p>
+          <h3>Direction and speed are choices</h3>
+          <div class="posture-scale" role="img" aria-label="Four postures: run away, stand still, move toward, sprint">
+            <article class="posture-scale__step"><strong>Run away</strong><span>Reject the change</span><p>Preserve the old operating model and avoid the layer.</p></article>
+            <article class="posture-scale__step"><strong>Stand still</strong><span>Observe indefinitely</span><p>Consume tools and commentary without changing the system.</p></article>
+            <article class="posture-scale__step"><strong>Move toward</strong><span>Run bounded experiments</span><p>Instrument one class and improve its workflow.</p></article>
+            <article class="posture-scale__step posture-scale__step--sprint"><strong>Sprint</strong><span>Invest in compounding leverage</span><p>Build the layer, learn quickly, and expand only as evidence earns it.</p></article>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--story">
+          <p class="move-label">3 · Story of one move — compare two engineers</p>
+          <h3>One builds the layer; one waits inside every task</h3>
+          <p>From 38:55 to 40:40, Dan contrasts two futures. One engineer spends weeks investing in an agentic layer, then writes a prompt and lets a proven class flow toward production, with the no-issue rate rising as tools and models improve. The other opens Jira, prompts in-loop, sits, waits, iterates, and retests. Dan asks who wins and who makes the business more money.</p>
+          <p>The comparison is about compounding. Better models improve the engineer who built reliable context, tools, prompts, environments, and feedback loops without requiring the whole operating system to be rediscovered. At 41:35 Dan says the goal is not to be right about every forecast but to be prepared to win. Preparation is encoded capability.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--name">
+          <p class="move-label">4 · Name it — belief as investment thesis</p>
+          <h3>Belief in the agentic layer</h3>
+          <aside class="callout callout--term">
+            <p class="callout__label">Not blind confidence</p>
+            <p><strong>Belief:</strong> the conviction that isolated environments, reusable prompts, state, loops, evidence, and composition are worth engineering because future model/tool gains will multiply through them.</p>
+          </aside>
+          <p>Belief is falsifiable here. You invest in one workflow, record presence and failures, and see whether the layer pays a dividend. The opposite of belief is not caution; it is refusing to build the experimental surface on which confidence could become evidence.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--example">
+          <p class="move-label">5 · Worked example — the first week after the course</p>
+          <h3>One chore class, one ledger, one decision</h3>
+          <p>Choose a course-code chore such as bounded UI copy. Define its route and proof. Run it reviewed three times in the course environment. Record attempts, findings, and presence. If review adds no value and the pipeline gates behave as designed, run one uppercase-ZTE experiment. Inspect the evidence afterward and decide whether the next same-class run stays ZTE, returns out-loop, or needs a stronger check.</p>
+          <p>This example remains within the course codebase as required. Its purpose is to turn the closing speech into a repeatable method: invest, instrument, decide, improve.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--try">
+          <p class="move-label">6 · You try — write the next irreversible-looking step reversibly</p>
+          <h3>A seven-day agentic-layer bet</h3>
+          <p>Complete this sentence: “For the next seven days, I will target lower presence for ___ in tac-7 by adding or exercising ___; I will keep human review until ___; the experiment fails if ___.” Keep the scope small enough that failure teaches rather than harms. Name the artifact—test, screenshot, state check, or KPI—that will decide the next move.</p>
+          <p>Pass if the bet can end in “return to out-loop” without embarrassment.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--fail">
+          <p class="move-label">7 · Common fail + fix</p>
+          <h3>Failure: substituting conviction for controls</h3>
+          <p><strong>Symptom:</strong> “I believe agents can run the codebase” becomes permission to skip tests, evidence, or class boundaries.</p>
+          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> belief funds the layer; it does not waive the layer. Sprinting means building faster feedback and stronger composition, not expanding authority faster than evidence. The engineer prepared to win can state both the North Star and the next abort condition.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--current">
+          <p class="move-label">8 · Current practice overlay</p>
+          <h3>The tool curve rewards prepared codebases</h3>
+          <aside class="current-practice">
+            <p class="current-practice__label">Durable close</p>
+            <p>Native isolation, SDKs, skills, and stronger models reduce the cost of building the layer. They do not supply your problem classes, review contracts, domain state, or shipping policy. A prepared codebase can adopt new primitives into an existing system; an unprepared one receives a better chat interface and keeps the human as glue.</p>
+            <p class="current-practice__source">The chapter uses exactly three public-video overlays, reserved for the three named plumbing shifts: isolation, SDK embedding, and Skills.</p>
+          </aside>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--check">
+          <p class="move-label">9 · Retrieval check</p>
+          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Belief and posture retrieval questions">
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. Name the four postures.</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Run away, stand still, move toward, and sprint.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. What does “prepared to win” mean operationally?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Build reusable context, prompts, tools, isolation, state, loops, evidence, and compositions so new model/tool improvements multiply through a working layer.</p></details></div>
+            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. What is the one concrete ask after the motivation?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Choose a bounded problem class, encode and instrument its workflow, run an experiment, and let evidence determine whether presence can fall.</p></details></div>
+          </section>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="move move--teach">
+          <p class="move-label">10 · Teach-back prompt</p>
+          <div class="teach-back">
+            <p><strong>Give the chapter’s closing argument without futurism.</strong> Compare the two engineers in terms of repeated transitions and compounding infrastructure, then propose one seven-day, course-code experiment with a metric and abort condition.</p>
+            <p class="fine">Pass if your listener leaves with an action rather than a slogan.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter-section chapter-section--wide" id="lab">
+        <span class="section-number">13</span>
+        <h2 class="section-title">Graded capstone · earn one zero-touch chore</h2>
+        <p class="section-lead">This 2–3 hour lab uses only the TAC-7 course codebase. You will run a reviewed SDLC, deliberately step back in-loop, prove selective heavy compute, compare patch with SDLC, execute one uppercase-ZTE chore, verify the lowercase interlock, read the KPIs, and clean every environment.</p>
+
+        <aside class="callout callout--warning">
+          <p class="callout__label">Authority, cost, and scope</p>
+          <p>Use a clone of <code>https://github.com/disler/tac-7.git</code> pushed to a GitHub repository you control. Review, patch, and ship create issue comments, PRs, and a merge; do not aim them at a repository you do not own. Multiple full agent runs and one heavy model-set run can incur meaningful token cost. Set a budget before starting. Type zero application-code lines by hand.</p>
+        </aside>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-setup">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 0 · Establish the course environment and evidence surfaces</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>20 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 0</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Failure this exposes:</strong> launching an auto-merge workflow before you know which repository it controls, what the app’s healthy state looks like, or where run evidence lands.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Clone and enter the course repository:
+              <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/disler/tac-7.git
+cd tac-7</code></pre>
+            </li>
+            <li>Create an empty GitHub repository you control. Repoint this course clone and push main:
+              <pre><code>git remote remove origin
+git remote add origin &lt;your-repository-url&gt;
+git push -u origin main</code></pre>
+              <p>Verify <code>git remote -v</code>. Do not proceed until every push destination is yours.</p>
+            </li>
+            <li>Copy <code>.env.sample</code> to <code>.env</code>. Configure <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>, export <code>GITHUB_REPO_URL</code>, and configure the NL-to-SQL application’s key in <code>app/server/.env</code>. Cloudflare R2 variables are optional; without them, review returns local screenshot paths.</li>
+            <li>Verify tools:
+              <pre><code>claude --version
+gh auth status
+uv --version
+bun --version
+git --version</code></pre>
+            </li>
+            <li>Boot the course app, upload sample data, run one natural-language query, and stop it:
+              <pre><code>./scripts/start.sh
+# inspect http://localhost:5173
+./scripts/stop_apps.sh</code></pre>
+            </li>
+            <li>Read <code>.claude/settings.json</code> and <code>adws/README.md</code>. Then locate the source files the chapter audits: <code>adws/adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py</code>, <code>adws/adw_ship_iso.py</code>, <code>adws/adw_modules/state.py</code>, <code>adws/adw_modules/worktree_ops.py</code>, and <code>adws/adw_modules/agent.py</code>.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 0 · say the three evidence locations.</strong> Run state/logs live under <code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/</code>; the isolated application lives under <code>trees/&lt;adw_id&gt;/</code>; human-facing reports, screenshots/paths, and PR links land on GitHub.</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>The remote points only to a repository you control.</li><li>All required tools respond and GitHub authentication is healthy.</li><li>The application works before agent changes.</li><li>You can name the three evidence surfaces and why ship requires your own repository.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-sdlc">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 1 · One issue becomes a production-shaped reviewed run</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>30 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 1</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Issue:</strong> <code>Add a footer showing the current table count. adw_sdlc_iso</code></p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Create the issue in your repository and record its number. Run the orchestrator manually first:
+              <pre><code>cd adws
+uv run adw_sdlc_iso.py &lt;issue-number&gt;</code></pre>
+            </li>
+            <li>When the ADW ID appears, open a second terminal and enter the clone’s <code>adws/</code> directory. Inspect isolation without editing:
+              <pre><code>git worktree list
+cat ../trees/&lt;adw_id&gt;/.ports.env
+cat ../agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/adw_state.json</code></pre>
+            </li>
+            <li>Name all ten state fields aloud. Mark which eight the ship workflow requires.</li>
+            <li>Open <code>trees/&lt;adw_id&gt;</code> in an editor only to observe the branch and live run. You are a guest in the agent’s environment. Do not alter files.</li>
+            <li>After completion, audit the issue thread in order: classification, state/plan, test report, review summary with at least one screenshot or local path, documentation, and PR. Open the PR diff.</li>
+            <li>Record presence honestly. For this reviewed run, the intended best case is prompt + review = 2.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 1 · trace the baton.</strong> Point to where issue number, branch name, plan path, class, worktree, ports, model set, and workflow history entered state. Explain how a fresh review agent found the correct environment without receiving the planner’s transcript.</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>The issue thread forms a complete paper trail through PR.</li><li>Worktree, branch, and ports are visible and unique.</li><li>All ten state fields are explained accurately.</li><li>Review includes at least one proof image or expected local path.</li><li>No application code was hand-edited.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-parallel">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 1B · Prove two ADWs are isolated while concurrent</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15–25 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 1B</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Failure this exposes:</strong> calling the environment “parallel-ready” after observing only one worktree at a time.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Create two independent, low-risk chore issues in the course clone. To limit compute, you may use two concurrent <code>adw_plan_iso.py</code> runs; the isolation proof does not require two additional full SDLCs.</li>
+            <li>Launch the two issue-number runs in separate terminals before either completes.</li>
+            <li>While both are live, capture <code>git worktree list</code>, both <code>trees/&lt;adw_id&gt;/.ports.env</code> files, and both <code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/adw_state.json</code> paths.</li>
+            <li>Compare four ownership dimensions: worktree path, branch name, backend/frontend port pair, and state directory. All must be disjoint.</li>
+            <li>Record one sentence explaining why one agent can reset or serve its application without changing the other agent’s environment.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 1B · four-part proof.</strong> Two simultaneous ADWs have different worktrees, branches, port pairs, and state locations. Terminal count alone is not evidence.</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>Both ADWs overlap in time.</li><li>Evidence captures two registered worktrees.</li><li>Branch, ports, and state are disjoint.</li><li>The four-part isolation explanation is correct.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-in-loop">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 2 · Step back in-loop on purpose</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 2</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Failure this exposes:</strong> treating the velocity scale as a one-way identity instead of a reversible operating choice.</p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>In an interactive Claude Code session, run:
+              <pre><code>/in_loop_review &lt;branch-name&gt;</code></pre>
+            </li>
+            <li>Let the command fetch and check out the branch, prepare the app, and open localhost. Exercise the footer yourself.</li>
+            <li>Write one sentence: “My in-loop review caught ___.” If it caught nothing, write “nothing” rather than inventing value.</li>
+            <li>Add this touch to your presence ledger and state whether the run moved from out-loop into in-loop during training.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 2 · explain the gradient.</strong> “I chose to add a human touch because ___; it did/did not change the outcome; repeated evidence of no added value would justify ___ for this class.”</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>You exercised the real feature in the browser.</li><li>Your finding sentence is honest and specific.</li><li>You can explain why moving backward on the scale can be correct.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-heavy">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 3 · Turn the compute knob and prove selection</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>20 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 3</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Issue:</strong> <code>Generate random rows for a selected table. adw_sdlc_iso model_set heavy</code></p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Create and run the issue using the same reviewed SDLC path.</li>
+            <li>Confirm <code>"model_set": "heavy"</code> in its state file.</li>
+            <li>Inspect <code>agents/&lt;adw_id&gt;/sdlc_implementor/raw_output.jsonl</code> for the model field, or use the issue’s “Model Set” report.</li>
+            <li>Collect evidence that implementation used the heavy-map value while classification, test, and review stayed on the base-map value in this course source.</li>
+            <li>Read <code>SLASH_COMMAND_MODEL_MAP</code> and independently count the eight entries whose base and heavy values differ.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 3 · one policy sentence.</strong> “Heavy selects a per-command policy stored in state; it does not assign one heavy model to every phase.”</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>State proves the heavy set was selected.</li><li>Run evidence proves implementation escalated.</li><li>At least one structural phase is proven to remain base.</li><li>You can name all eight escalating commands or derive them from source.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-patch">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 4 · Compare patch composition with full SDLC</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 4</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Issue:</strong> <code>Rename the Query button to Run. adw_patch_iso</code></p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Create the issue and run the patch workflow.</li>
+            <li>Find its plan beneath <code>specs/patch/</code>.</li>
+            <li>List the phases that ran: isolated worktree, targeted patch planning, implementation, and PR.</li>
+            <li>List the full-SDLC phases that did not run: the full plan/test/review/document ceremony.</li>
+            <li>Compare run duration, artifacts, and presence with Lab 1 without claiming that shorter automatically means better.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 4 · secret in miniature.</strong> Explain why patch is a different composition of shared primitives for “change one thing,” not merely a deficient SDLC.</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>The targeted patch plan and PR exist.</li><li>You identify included and omitted phases correctly.</li><li>You can defend the routing choice by problem class.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-zte">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 5 · Exercise the ZTE ceremony and uppercase interlock</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>30 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 5</li></ul>
+          <p><strong>Uppercase issue:</strong> <code>/chore adw_sdlc_ZTE_iso - change the background color to light green</code></p>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Before launching, write the class boundary, required proof, abort conditions, and predicted presence.</li>
+            <li>Create the uppercase issue. In an interactive Claude Code session, run <code>/classify_adw</code> with the raw issue text and confirm that the returned route is ZTE. Then launch the returned course orchestrator manually:
+              <pre><code>cd adws
+uv run adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py &lt;issue-number&gt;</code></pre>
+              <p>Capture the six-phase initiation comment and automatic-merge warning. The script filename is lowercase; the safety interlock applies to the <em>issue token</em> interpreted by <code>/classify_adw</code>.</p>
+            </li>
+            <li>Watch the phase status without intervening. If test or review fails, count the run as an honest abort; do not bypass the gate to manufacture a pass.</li>
+            <li>After success, update your local main and inspect the transition:
+              <pre><code>git pull
+git log --oneline -3</code></pre>
+              <p>Look for a merge commit whose message includes <code>via ADW Ship workflow</code>.</p>
+            </li>
+            <li>Serve main and verify the requested light-green background.</li>
+            <li>Create a second issue using lowercase <code>adw_sdlc_zte_iso</code>. Run <code>/classify_adw</code> with that raw text and capture its reviewed <code>adw_sdlc_iso</code> route. If you continue the second run manually, launch <code>uv run adw_sdlc_iso.py &lt;issue-number&gt;</code>; do <em>not</em> bypass classification by calling the ZTE script. Do not call the downgrade a bug.</li>
+            <li>Read <code>adw_sdlc_zte_iso.py</code> and prove <code>--skip-e2e</code> is always appended. Record clearly that browser E2E did not gate this ZTE run.</li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 5 · count before you trust.</strong> Recite: explicit uppercase route → test pass → review pass → eight complete state fields → three-way worktree validation → fetch/checkout/pull/<code>merge --no-ff</code>/push with branch restoration attempts.</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>One uppercase run auto-merges with no human approval touch, or an honest gate abort is diagnosed and rerun without bypassing.</li><li>The merge commit and visible main-branch change are proven.</li><li>The lowercase run visibly downgrades.</li><li>You disclose that browser E2E was skipped.</li><li>Your presence ledger is honest.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-kpi">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Lab 6 · Read the KPIs, close the loop, and reclaim disk</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>15 minutes</li><li>Required</li><li>Checkpoint 6</li></ul>
+          <ol class="lab-box__steps">
+            <li>Open <code>app_docs/agentic_kpis.md</code>. For each run, compare its row with issue class, plan, diff, and presence ledger.</li>
+            <li>For one state file, count <code>all_adws</code> entries by hand and compare with attempts. Recompute the current streak using your completed rows.</li>
+            <li>Write which problem class you would trust for the next ZTE experiment and which class you would keep reviewed.</li>
+            <li>Return to the repository root and purge every worktree:
+              <pre><code>./scripts/purge_tree.sh &lt;adw_id&gt;
+git worktree list</code></pre>
+              <p>Repeat for every run. Do not finish with stale course worktrees.</p>
+            </li>
+          </ol>
+          <div class="checkpoint"><strong>Checkpoint 6 · evidence-based decision.</strong> Your class choice must cite attempts, review findings, streak, and presence—not general confidence in agents.</div>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Pass criteria</h4><ul><li>KPI arithmetic matches the run records you inspected.</li><li>Presence is recorded for Labs 1, 3, 4, and 5.</li><li>The next-class decision cites evidence.</li><li><code>git worktree list</code> contains no stale lab worktrees.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-stretch">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Stretch drills · deepen the source audit</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>30–60 minutes</li><li>S2–S8</li><li>Course codebase only</li></ul>
+
+          <h4>S2 · Port-collision drill</h4>
+          <p>Occupy a deterministic course port with <code>python3 -m http.server 9107</code> before launching a run whose initial pair uses that index. Find where <code>find_next_available_ports</code> selects the next free pair. Stop the temporary server when complete.</p>
+
+          <h4>S3 · Break ship’s state gate</h4>
+          <p>Run <code>adw_plan_iso</code> only, then call <code>adw_ship_iso &lt;issue&gt; &lt;adw_id&gt;</code>. Capture the abort and list the exact missing fields. The goal is to prove the merge cannot start from a merely existing state file.</p>
+
+          <h4>S4 · Documentation-versus-source audit</h4>
+          <p>Compare the README’s “squash” description with <code>manual_merge_to_main</code>. Write three sentences explaining the real <code>--no-ff</code> path and the <code>git log</code> evidence that distinguishes the strategies.</p>
+
+          <h4>S5 · Latent cleanup bug</h4>
+          <p>Find the <code>shutil.rmtree</code> fallback in <code>worktree_ops.remove_worktree</code> and confirm that <code>shutil</code> is not imported. Explain the exact failure path and why it does not invalidate the three-way pre-phase validator.</p>
+
+          <h4>S6 · E2E honesty check</h4>
+          <p>After proving both SDLC orchestrators hardcode <code>--skip-e2e</code>, run the standalone test workflow without the flag:</p>
+          <pre><code>cd adws
+uv run adw_test_iso.py &lt;issue-number&gt; &lt;adw-id&gt;</code></pre>
+          <p>Playwright MCP must be configured. Compare this evidence with the earlier unit-test-only gate.</p>
+
+          <h4>S7 · Extend proof toward video</h4>
+          <p>Read <code>playwright-mcp-config.json</code>, confirm 1920×1080 recording into <code>./videos</code>, locate a review video beneath the worktree, and post the path or an uploaded URL to your issue. Do not invent a URL when only a local path exists.</p>
+
+          <h4>S8 · Wire a real trigger</h4>
+          <p>Run <code>uv run adw_triggers/trigger_webhook.py</code> on port 8001 and <code>scripts/expose_webhook.sh</code>, then configure Issues and Issue-comment events for your repository. File a course-code issue from another device and remain out-loop. The cron alternative polls every 20 seconds via <code>trigger_cron.py</code>.</p>
+
+          <div class="lab-box__pass"><h4>Stretch pass criteria</h4><ul><li>S2 demonstrates availability fallback.</li><li>S3 aborts before Git and names missing fields.</li><li>S4 states <code>--no-ff</code> accurately.</li><li>S5 identifies the missing import without changing course application code.</li><li>S6 distinguishes unit from browser E2E evidence.</li><li>S7 preserves the real video path/URL.</li><li>S8 demonstrates issue-triggered execution in a repository you control.</li></ul></div>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-troubleshooting">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Troubleshooting · preserve the learning signal</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>Use when stuck</li><li>Diagnose from evidence</li></ul>
+          <table>
+            <thead><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Likely explanation</th><th>Repair or interpretation</th></tr></thead>
+            <tbody>
+              <tr><td>Dependent workflow demands an ADW ID</td><td>Review/document/ship need prior state</td><td>Copy the ID from planning output; do not start a disconnected run.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Review posts file paths instead of images</td><td>R2 variables are absent</td><td>Use local paths as expected evidence or configure all four R2 values.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Lowercase ZTE does not merge</td><td>The uppercase interlock worked</td><td>Read the classification output; use explicit <code>ZTE</code> only for the authorized lab step.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Ship says fields are missing</td><td>An upstream phase did not populate complete state</td><td>Read the named fields and trace the responsible phase. Do not hand-edit state to bypass.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>ZTE tests pass but browser behavior fails</td><td>SDLC always skipped browser E2E</td><td>Run standalone <code>adw_test_iso.py</code> without <code>--skip-e2e</code>; improve the class contract.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Cleanup fallback throws a name error</td><td><code>shutil</code> is unimported in source</td><td>Use the purge script/Git cleanup path and record the source defect; do not hide stale worktrees.</td></tr>
+              <tr><td>Costs rise during heavy/full runs</td><td>This is the course’s highest-compute capstone</td><td>Stop at your precommitted budget and preserve partial evidence; budget failure is not a ZTE correctness result.</td></tr>
+            </tbody>
+          </table>
+        </section>
+
+        <section class="lab-box" id="lab-final-pass">
+          <h3 class="lab-box__title">Final pass sheet · grade from artifacts</h3>
+          <ul class="lab-box__meta"><li>All required</li><li>Oral + operational</li></ul>
+          <div class="lab-box__pass">
